(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["kaivalya"]={"slug":"kaivalya","name":"Kaivalya","deva":"कैवल्य","note":"","intro":["A short, luminous <strong>Śaiva Upaniṣad</strong> of twenty-four verses in two <em>khaṇḍas</em>, counted <strong>12th in the Muktikā list of 108</strong>. It is the most compact and complete <strong>manual of Advaita by way of Śiva</strong>: the householder-sage <strong>Āśvalāyana</strong> approaches <strong>Brahmā the Parameṣṭhin</strong> and asks for <em>brahmavidyā</em>; the answer is not ritual but <strong>śraddhā–bhakti–dhyāna-yoga</strong> — faith, devotion, meditation — practised by one who has renounced, seated alone, who meditates in the <strong>lotus of the heart</strong> upon <strong>Umā's consort, the three-eyed, blue-throated, all-witnessing Parameśvara</strong> — and who then discovers that this God is his <strong>own Self</strong>. The text moves in a single arc from <strong>worship</strong> (vv. 5–7) through <strong>identification</strong> (vv. 8–19) to the great first-person hymn of <strong>\"I am\"</strong> (vv. 20–23), closing with the <strong>Śatarudriya phala-śruti</strong> and the word that names it: <em>kaivalyaṃ padam aśnute</em> — \"he attains the state of Aloneness.\"","Its distinction is to take <em>kaivalya</em>, the <strong>Sāṃkhya-Yoga</strong> word for the puruṣa's isolation from prakṛti (<em>Yoga-sūtra</em> 4.34), and re-mint it as the <strong>Advaitic Aloneness of the one Self</strong> — \"there being no second.\" It is, with the Śvetāśvatara, the classical bridge between <strong>Vedānta and Śaiva theology</strong>, and the source of some of the most quoted lines in later Vedānta: <em>sa brahmā sa śivaḥ sendraḥ</em> (8), <em>tattvam eva tvam eva tat</em> (16), <em>mayy eva sakalaṃ jātam</em> (19), <em>aṇor aṇīyān aham eva</em> (20), and <em>cinmātro 'haṃ sadāśivaḥ</em> (18)."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"Kaivalya Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<strong>कैवल्य उपनिषत्</strong> — <em>\"The Upaniṣad of Aloneness (kaivalya) — Absolute Independence\"</em>","A short, luminous <strong>Śaiva Upaniṣad</strong> of twenty-four verses in two <em>khaṇḍas</em>, counted <strong>12th in the Muktikā list of 108</strong>. It is the most compact and complete <strong>manual of Advaita by way of Śiva</strong>: the householder-sage <strong>Āśvalāyana</strong> approaches <strong>Brahmā the Parameṣṭhin</strong> and asks for <em>brahmavidyā</em>; the answer is not ritual but <strong>śraddhā–bhakti–dhyāna-yoga</strong> — faith, devotion, meditation — practised by one who has renounced, seated alone, who meditates in the <strong>lotus of the heart</strong> upon <strong>Umā's consort, the three-eyed, blue-throated, all-witnessing Parameśvara</strong> — and who then discovers that this God is his <strong>own Self</strong>. The text moves in a single arc from <strong>worship</strong> (vv. 5–7) through <strong>identification</strong> (vv. 8–19) to the great first-person hymn of <strong>\"I am\"</strong> (vv. 20–23), closing with the <strong>Śatarudriya phala-śruti</strong> and the word that names it: <em>kaivalyaṃ padam aśnute</em> — \"he attains the state of Aloneness.\"","Its distinction is to take <em>kaivalya</em>, the <strong>Sāṃkhya-Yoga</strong> word for the puruṣa's isolation from prakṛti (<em>Yoga-sūtra</em> 4.34), and re-mint it as the <strong>Advaitic Aloneness of the one Self</strong> — \"there being no second.\" It is, with the Śvetāśvatara, the classical bridge between <strong>Vedānta and Śaiva theology</strong>, and the source of some of the most quoted lines in later Vedānta: <em>sa brahmā sa śivaḥ sendraḥ</em> (8), <em>tattvam eva tvam eva tat</em> (16), <em>mayy eva sakalaṃ jātam</em> (19), <em>aṇor aṇīyān aham eva</em> (20), and <em>cinmātro 'haṃ sadāśivaḥ</em> (18).","Sanskrit text follows the standard (vulgate) recension, verified verse-by-verse from the ITRANS edition of <strong>sanskritdocuments.org</strong> (`kaivalya.itx`, transliterated and proofread by Sunder Hattangadi, last updated 2023), rendered to Devanāgarī and IAST with the `indic_transliteration` (sanscript) library. Cross-referenced against <strong>K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar</strong>, <em>Thirty Minor Upanishads</em> (1914) and <strong>Paul Deussen</strong>, <em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em> (1897).","⚠️ <em>Accuracy note:</em> the <em>mūla</em> below is the vulgate. Recensions differ in four places recorded here (1 <em>yayā/yathā</em>; 5 <em>atyāśrama/antyāśrama</em>; 11 <em>pāpaṃ/pāśaṃ</em>; 20 <em>purātano/purāṇo</em>), and editions divide the closing material as <strong>24</strong> or <strong>25</strong> verses. Two evident e-text slips have been silently corrected against the printed editions: v. 4 `suniśrcitārthāḥ` → <strong>suniścitārthāḥ</strong>, v. 19 `tad.hbrahma` → <strong>tad brahma</strong>. Readers doing close textual work should spot-check against the Adyar Library edition of the 108 with the <em>Dīpikā</em>."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The text at a glance","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Khaṇḍa</td><td>Verses</td><td>Theme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>1–19</td><td><strong>From worship to identity.</strong> Āśvalāyana's question and Brahmā's answer (<strong>śraddhā, bhakti, dhyāna</strong>, not works, 1–2); renunciation, not <em>karma</em>, as the gate (3–4); the <strong>posture and the heart-lotus</strong> (5–6); the meditation on <strong>Umā-sahāya Parameśvara</strong> (7); <strong>\"He is Brahmā, He is Śiva\"</strong> — the one God as all gods (8–9); seeing the Self in all beings (10); the <strong>araṇi of knowledge</strong> (11); the <strong>three states</strong> — waking, dream, deep sleep — and the <strong>three cities</strong> in which the <em>jīva</em> plays (12–14); the world's emanation from That (15); <strong><em>tat tvam asi</em></strong> restated (16); the Witness beyond the three states (17–18); and the seal: <strong>\"In me alone all is born\"</strong> (19).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>20–24</td><td><strong>The hymn of \"I am\" and the fruit.</strong> <em>Aṇor aṇīyān aham eva</em> — \"I am subtler than the subtle, I am the vast\" (20); the <strong>organless knower</strong> — seeing without eyes, hearing without ears (21); <strong>\"By all the Vedas am I to be known\"</strong> (22); the negation of the five elements and the entry into the <strong>pure supreme Self</strong> (23); the <strong>Śatarudriya phala-śruti</strong>, <strong>Avimukta</strong> (Kāśī), the <em>atyāśramin</em>, and the twice-spoken close — <strong><em>kaivalyaṃ padam aśnute</em></strong> (24).</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The five commentaries used in this volume","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin — <em>Kaivalyopaniṣad-dīpikā</strong></em> (Rāmacandrendra Sarasvatī, 18th c., Kāñcī). The only commentator to have glossed all 108 Upaniṣads in Sanskrit, and the standard classical gloss for the minor Upaniṣads; a thoroughgoing <strong>Advaitin</strong> who reads every Śaiva name as the <em>nirguṇa</em> Self under a form. Adyar Library edition. Cited as <strong>UBY</strong>.","2. <strong>Śaṅkarānanda — <em>Kaivalyopaniṣad-dīpikā</strong></em> (14th c.), the concise, word-focused Advaita gloss of the <em>dīpikā</em> tradition. <em>(Several printed editions circulate a Kaivalya commentary under the name of <strong>Śaṅkarācārya</strong> himself; modern scholarship does not accept that ascription, and the gloss is close to Śaṅkarānanda's.)</em> Cited as <strong>Śaṅkarānanda</strong>.","3. <strong>Swāmī Chinmayānanda — <em>Kaivalya Upaniṣad</strong></em> (Chinmaya Mission; discourses and commentary). The best-known modern exposition of this text, reading it as a <strong>complete sādhanā manual</strong> — qualification, seat, meditation, identification, and the <em>jīvanmukta</em>'s \"I am.\" Cited as <strong>Chinmayānanda</strong>.","4. <strong>Swāmī Sivānanda &amp; Swāmī Krishnānanda (Divine Life Society)</strong> — the practical yoga-and-devotion lens; the text as instructions for the seat, the heart-lotus, the <em>praṇava</em>-churning, and the <em>sākṣī-bhāva</em>. Cited as <strong>Sivānanda/Krishnānanda</strong>.","5. <strong>Paul Deussen (1897), K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar (1914) &amp; Patrick Olivelle (1992, on <em>saṃnyāsa</em> Upaniṣads)</strong> — the Indological / text-critical lens: dating, the Atharvan-vs-Yajurvedic attribution, the borrowings from Muṇḍaka, Śvetāśvatara, Kaṭha and the Mahānārāyaṇa, and the renunciant milieu. Cited as <strong>Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle</strong>.","<em>On usage below:</em> under each verse, <strong>UBY</strong> is the classical Sanskrit gloss, <strong>Śaṅkarānanda</strong> the close Advaita word-reading, <strong>Chinmayānanda</strong> the modern expository/sādhanā reading, <strong>Sivānanda/Krishnānanda</strong> the contemplative application, <strong>Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle</strong> the text-critical and comparative angle. Where a commentator is silent on a verse, the entry gives the reading his system requires rather than a quotation."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Veda, school, and the two attributions","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Question</td><td>The evidence</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Which Veda?</strong></td><td>The <strong>colophon</strong> of the vulgate reads <em>ity <strong>atharvavedīyā</strong> kaivalyopaniṣat samāptā</em> — \"thus ends the Kaivalya Upaniṣad <strong>of the Atharvaveda</strong>.\" But the <strong>Muktikā</strong> classification and most manuscript catalogues assign it to the <strong>Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda</strong>, and its <strong>śānti-pāṭha is <em>saha nāvavatu</strong></em>, the Taittirīya/Kaṭha peace-invocation of the Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda — not an Atharvan one. Both labels are therefore attested; the Yajurvedic affiliation is the stronger internal evidence, the Atharvan the stronger colophonic.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Which class?</strong></td><td>One of the <strong>Śaiva Upaniṣads</strong> (with the Atharvaśiras, Atharvaśikha, Kālāgnirudra, Nīlarudra, Bṛhajjābāla, Rudrahṛdaya, Bhasmajābāla, Rudrākṣajābāla, Gaṇapati, Pañcabrahma, Akṣamālikā, Śarabha, Dakṣiṇāmūrti, and Jābāli), and simultaneously a <strong>saṃnyāsa</strong> text: vv. 3–5 make renunciation the qualification.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Muktikā number</strong></td><td><strong>12</strong> of the 108.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Date</strong></td><td>Post-Muṇḍaka and post-Śvetāśvatara (it quotes both), pre-Purāṇic in idiom. Deussen places it among the later Atharvan/Yajurvedic Upaniṣads; a conventional range is roughly the <strong>last centuries BCE to the early centuries CE</strong>, before the classical Śaiva Āgamas.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Speakers</strong></td><td><strong>Āśvalāyana</strong> (the <em>ṛṣi</em> of the Ṛgvedic <em>Āśvalāyana-Śrautasūtra</em> lineage) asks; <strong>Parameṣṭhin / Pitāmaha</strong> — <strong>Brahmā</strong> — answers. The frame is deliberately the highest available: the grandfather of the worlds teaches the <em>brahmavidyā</em>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Title</strong></td><td><strong><em>Kaivalya</em></strong> — \"aloneness, sole-ness, absolute independence,\" from <em>kevala</em>, \"alone, one only, entire.\" In <strong>Sāṃkhya-Yoga</strong> it names the puruṣa's isolation from prakṛti (<em>Yoga-sūtra</em> 2.25, 4.34); here it is re-read as the <strong>non-dual Aloneness</strong> of the one Self — <em>tad brahmādvayam asmy aham</em> (19).</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Śānti-pāṭha (Peace invocation of the Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda)","title":"","sa":["ॐ सह नाववतु । सह नौ भुनक्तु । सह वीर्यं करवावहै ।","तेजस्वि नावधीतमस्तु । मा विद्विषावहै ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ saha nāvavatu | saha nau bhunaktu | saha vīryaṃ karavāvahai |","tejasvi nāvadhītam astu | mā vidviṣāvahai ||","oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"<strong>May He protect us both; may He nourish us both; may we work together with vigour; may our study be illumining; may we never hate one another.</strong> Oṃ, peace, peace, peace.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"Maṅgala (the traditional dhyāna-śloka prefixed to the text)","title":"","sa":["कैवल्योपनिषद्वेद्यं कैवल्यानन्दतुन्दिलम् ।","कैवल्यगिरिजारामं स्वमात्रं कलयेऽन्वहम् ॥"],"iast":["kaivalyopaniṣadvedyaṃ kaivalyānandatundilam |","kaivalyagirijārāmaṃ svamātraṃ kalaye 'nvaham ||"],"words":[],"lit":"<strong>\"Day after day I contemplate That which is to be known by the Kaivalya Upaniṣad, swollen full with the bliss of Aloneness, the delight of Girijā (Pārvatī) who is Aloneness itself — That which is nothing but my own Self.\"</strong>","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The verse is a miniature of the whole book: <em>kaivalya</em> four times over — as <strong>text</strong>, as <strong>bliss</strong>, as the <strong>God with His consort</strong>, and finally as <strong><em>sva-mātra</em>, \"nothing but oneself.\"</strong>"]}]},{"t":"The twenty-four verses","u":[{"ref":"The twenty-four verses","title":"Overview","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Each verse is given as: <strong>Devanāgarī → IAST → pada/phrase gloss → literal translation → paraphrase → 5-commentary digest.</strong> Each <em>khaṇḍa</em> closes with three applied sections — <strong>Details</strong> (deeper exposition), <strong>Visual representation</strong> (diagram), and <strong>Practical steps / observations</strong> (contemplative application).","<em>Grammar abbreviations:</em> m./f./n. = masc./fem./neut.; nom./acc./instr./dat./abl./gen./loc./voc. = case; sg./du./pl. = number; pres./impf./pf./aor./pot./impv./ger./inf. = tense/mood/non-finite; pp. = past participle; abs. = absolutive (gerund in <em>-tvā/-ya</em>)."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Khaṇḍa 1 — The Question, the Meditation, and the Identity","u":[{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 1","title":"The Question, the Meditation, and the Identity","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The first khaṇḍa is the body of the teaching. A qualified seeker asks the highest god for the highest knowledge; he is told at once that its means are</em> <strong>faith, devotion, and meditation</strong> <em>(2), and that the gate is</em> <strong>renunciation, not works</strong> <em>(3–4). Then comes the practice in its classical order —</em> <strong>solitary seat, erect posture, sense-restraint, salutation to the guru</strong> <em>(5);</em> <strong>the heart-lotus</strong> <em>as the place (6); and</em> <strong>Umā's consort, the three-eyed blue-throated Lord</strong>, <em>as the object (7). The moment the meditation is described, the text begins to dissolve the distance between worshipper and worshipped:</em> <strong>He is Brahmā, Śiva, Indra, Viṣṇu, prāṇa, time, fire, moon</strong> <em>(8–9);</em> <strong>see the Self in all beings</strong> <em>(10);</em> <strong>churn out knowledge with the praṇava</strong> <em>(11). Then the great analysis of the</em> <strong>three states</strong> <em>(12–14) shows the one</em> jīva <em>wandering the</em> <strong>three cities</strong> <em>— and resolves them into their</em> <strong>ground, bliss, undivided awareness</strong> <em>. From That the world proceeds (15); and</em> <strong>\"That indeed art thou, thou indeed art That\"</strong> <em>(16). The khaṇḍa ends on the Witness (17–18) and the seal of non-duality (19).</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Verse 1","title":"Āśvalāyana's question","sa":["ॐ अथाश्वलायनो भगवन्तं परमेष्ठिनमुपसमेत्योवाच ।","अधीहि भगवन्ब्रह्मविद्यां वरिष्ठां सदा सद्भिः सेव्यमानां निगूढाम् ।","ययाऽचिरात्सर्वपापं व्यपोह्य परात्परं पुरुषं याति विद्वान् ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["oṃ athāśvalāyano bhagavantaṃ parameṣṭhinam upasametyovāca |","adhīhi bhagavan brahmavidyāṃ variṣṭhāṃ sadā sadbhiḥ sevyamānāṃ nigūḍhām |","yayā 'cirāt sarvapāpaṃ vyapohya parātparaṃ puruṣaṃ yāti vidvān || 1 ||"],"words":[["atha āśvalāyanaḥ","adv. + m. nom. sg.","then Āśvalāyana"],["bhagavantaṃ parameṣṭhinam","m. acc. sg. ×2","the Blessed One, the Highest-Stationed (Brahmā)"],["upasametya uvāca","abs. + pf. 3 sg.","having approached, said"],["adhīhi bhagavan","impv. 2 sg. (adhi-√i) + voc.","\"teach (me), O Lord,"],["brahma-vidyāṃ variṣṭhām","f. acc. sg. + superl.","the knowledge of Brahman, the most excellent,"],["sadā sadbhiḥ sevyamānām","adv. + m. instr. pl. + pass. pres. part. f. acc.","ever served (resorted to) by the good,"],["nigūḍhām","f. acc. pp.","(and yet) hidden (secret),"],["yayā acirāt","f. instr. sg. (rel.) + abl. adv.","by which, before long,"],["sarva-pāpaṃ vyapohya","n. acc. + abs. (vi-apa-√ūh)","having swept away all evil,"],["parāt-paraṃ puruṣaṃ yāti vidvān","abl.+acc. + acc. + pres. + m. nom.","the knower goes to the Person higher than the high.\""]],"lit":"\"Oṃ. Then Āśvalāyana, approaching the Blessed Parameṣṭhin, said: 'Teach me, Lord, the knowledge of Brahman — the most excellent, ever cultivated by the good, and (yet) hidden — by which the knower, quickly sweeping away all evil, goes to the Puruṣa higher than the highest.'\"","para":"The Upaniṣad opens with the <strong>classical act of approach</strong>: a qualified seeker comes <em>near and low</em> (<em>upa-sam-etya</em>) to a teacher and asks. Three adjectives fix what he wants: it is <strong>variṣṭhā</strong> (the highest of knowledges, not one among many), <strong>sad-bhiḥ sevyamānā</strong> (kept alive by a living lineage of the good — knowledge with a community, not private speculation), and <strong>nigūḍhā</strong> (hidden — not withheld out of meanness, but <em>structurally</em> concealed, since the knower can never be an object). Its fruit is stated at once and in the right order: first the <strong>sweeping away of evil</strong> (<em>sarva-pāpaṃ vyapohya</em>) — purification — and only then <strong>attainment of the Puruṣa \"higher than the high.\"</strong> Note that the teacher asked is <strong>Brahmā</strong> himself: for the Upaniṣadic imagination, <em>brahmavidyā</em> descends from the creator down through the lineage, and no lesser authority will do.","coms":[["UBY","<em>parātpara</em> = beyond even the \"high\" (<em>para</em>) — beyond Hiraṇyagarbha and the unmanifest; the object of the whole text. <em>Nigūḍhā</em> because it is <em>aparokṣa</em> (immediate) and therefore invisible to the objectifying mind."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>sadbhiḥ sevyamānā</em> establishes <em>sampradāya</em> — the vidyā is valid only as transmitted; <em>acirāt</em> promises that the removal of <em>pāpa</em> is not the labour of lives once knowledge dawns."],["Chinmayānanda","the student's question already contains the four qualifications (<em>sādhana-catuṣṭaya</em>): discrimination (he asks for the <em>highest</em>), dispassion (he wants <em>pāpa</em> removed, not prosperity), discipline (he approaches properly), and burning desire for liberation (<em>mumukṣutva</em>)."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","approach (<em>upasadana</em>) is itself the first practice — humility before a teacher is the condition of hearing at all."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","Āśvalāyana is the eponym of a Ṛgvedic ritual school; his appearance as the questioner in a Śaiva-Vedāntic text is a deliberate bid for orthodox authority. Variant <em>yathā</em> for <em>yayā</em> is attested."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 2","title":"The answer in four words","sa":["तस्मै स होवाच पितामहश्च श्रद्धाभक्तिध्यानयोगादवैहि ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["tasmai sa hovāca pitāmahaś ca śraddhā-bhakti-dhyāna-yogād avaihi || 2 ||"],"words":[["tasmai sa ha uvāca pitāmahaḥ ca","dat. sg. + nom. + pf. + m. nom.","and to him the Grandfather (Brahmā) said"],["śraddhā-bhakti-dhyāna-yogāt","m. abl. sg. (dvandva + yoga)","\"through the yoga of faith, devotion and meditation"],["avaihi","impv. 2 sg. (ava-√i)","know (It) / realise (It).\""]],"lit":"\"To him the Grandfather said: 'Know (It) through the yoga of faith, devotion and meditation.'\"","para":"The shortest answer in the Upaniṣads, and one of the most consequential. To a question about the <strong>highest knowledge</strong>, the reply names no ritual, no sacrifice, no lineage-privilege and no dialectic — only <strong>śraddhā</strong> (trusting conviction that steadies the mind before proof), <strong>bhakti</strong> (loving devotion to the Lord who will turn out to be one's Self), and <strong>dhyāna</strong> (sustained meditation), bound together as a single <strong><em>yoga</em></strong> (the ablative is singular: one discipline with three strands, not three options). The verb is <strong><em>ava-ihi</em></strong> — not \"believe,\" not \"infer,\" but <strong>\"realise, know down into\"</strong> (<em>ava-</em> = downward, thoroughly). That Brahmā himself prescribes <em>bhakti</em> as an organ of <em>jñāna</em> is the hinge on which this Upaniṣad, like Śvetāśvatara 6.23, turns.","coms":[["UBY","the compound is a <em>samāhāra</em>: the three are one means. <em>Śraddhā</em> steadies, <em>bhakti</em> purifies, <em>dhyāna</em> reveals; the <em>jñāna</em> that results is not produced by them but <em>uncovered</em>."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>avaihi</em> = <em>sākṣāt-kuru</em>, \"make it immediate.\" Faith and devotion are <em>antaraṅga</em> aids; the actual knowing is the mind's own final act."],["Chinmayānanda","the three correspond to the three faculties — <strong>intellect</strong> (śraddhā), <strong>emotion</strong> (bhakti), <strong>will</strong> (dhyāna). An integrated person meditates; a divided one only tries to."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","this single verse is the Divine Life programme in miniature: believe, love, meditate — and <em>know</em>."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","an early and unusually explicit subordination of <em>karma</em> to <em>bhakti-jñāna</em>; the verse is quoted in later Śaiva and Vedāntic anthologies as scriptural warrant for devotion as a path to knowledge."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 3","title":"Not by works, offspring or wealth","sa":["न कर्मणा न प्रजया धनेन त्यागेनैके अमृतत्वमानशुः ।","परेण नाकं निहितं गुहायां विभ्राजते यद्यतयो विशन्ति ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["na karmaṇā na prajayā dhanena tyāgenaike amṛtatvam ānaśuḥ |","pareṇa nākaṃ nihitaṃ guhāyāṃ vibhrājate yad yatayo viśanti || 3 ||"],"words":[["na karmaṇā na prajayā dhanena","n./f./n. instr. sg.","not by action, not by progeny, (not) by wealth"],["tyāgena eke amṛtatvam ānaśuḥ","instr. + nom. pl. + n. acc. + pf. 3 pl. (√aś)","by renunciation alone some have attained immortality"],["pareṇa nākam","instr./adv. + m. acc.","beyond heaven"],["nihitaṃ guhāyām","n. nom. pp. + f. loc.","set (deposited) in the cave (of the heart)"],["vibhrājate","pres. mid. 3 sg.","it shines forth"],["yat yatayaḥ viśanti","rel. n. + m. nom. pl. + pres. 3 pl.","which the strivers (ascetics) enter"]],"lit":"\"Not by action, not by progeny, (not) by wealth — by renunciation alone did some attain immortality. Beyond heaven, set in the cave (of the heart), it shines — that which the strivers enter.\"","para":"A famous verse, shared verbatim with the <strong>Mahānārāyaṇa Upaniṣad</strong> (and <em>Taittirīya Āraṇyaka</em> 10) and near-identical to the Kaivalya's own source-stratum. It closes off the three classical Vedic goods — <strong>karma</strong> (ritual action), <strong>prajā</strong> (sons, the social immortality of the line), and <strong>dhana</strong> (wealth) — precisely the <em>triad the Veda itself promises</em>. Against all three stands one word: <strong><em>tyāga</em>, letting-go.</strong> And the immortality gained is not a place \"beyond heaven\" in the sense of further away; the second line immediately relocates it: it is <strong>\"set in the cave\"</strong> — the heart — and it <strong>shines there already</strong> (<em>vibhrājate</em>, present tense). The <em>yatis</em> do not build it; they <strong>enter</strong> what is burning quietly all along. The \"beyond heaven\" and \"in the cave\" are the same address, read from outside and from inside.","coms":[["UBY","<em>tyāga</em> here is not mere abandonment of objects but <em>sarva-karma-saṃnyāsa</em> joined to knowledge; <em>nākam</em> is glossed as <em>svarga</em>, and <em>pareṇa nākam</em> marks the goal as beyond all attained worlds — hence not a result of action at all."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the three rejected means are the three <em>eṣaṇās</em> (cravings) — for ritual merit, for progeny, for wealth; each produces a <em>result</em>, and what is eternal cannot be a result."],["Chinmayānanda","the logic is airtight — anything <em>achieved</em> can be lost; immortality is therefore not achievable, only <strong>discovered by giving up the achiever</strong>."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","<em>tyāga</em> is inward: the renunciation of the sense of doership and possession, which a householder can practise as truly as a monk — though the Upaniṣad plainly honours the formal renunciate."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","the verse is a <em>locus classicus</em> of the renunciant movement and is cited in the <em>saṃnyāsa</em> Upaniṣads and by later Vedāntins; its presence here marks Kaivalya as a text of and for <em>saṃnyāsins</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 4","title":"Who is released, and when","sa":["वेदान्तविज्ञानसुनिश्चितार्थाः संन्यासयोगाद्यतयः शुद्धसत्त्वाः ।","ते ब्रह्मलोकेषु परान्तकाले परामृताः परिमुच्यन्ति सर्वे ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["vedānta-vijñāna-suniścitārthāḥ saṃnyāsa-yogād yatayaḥ śuddha-sattvāḥ |","te brahmalokeṣu parāntakāle parāmṛtāḥ parimucyanti sarve || 4 ||"],"words":[["vedānta-vijñāna-suniścita-arthāḥ","m. nom. pl. bahuvrīhi","those whose aim is well ascertained by the knowledge of Vedānta"],["saṃnyāsa-yogāt","m. abl. sg.","through the yoga of renunciation"],["yatayaḥ śuddha-sattvāḥ","m. nom. pl. + bahuvrīhi","strivers, of purified being (mind)"],["te brahma-lokeṣu","nom. pl. + m. loc. pl.","they, in the worlds of Brahman"],["para-anta-kāle","m. loc. sg.","at the time of the supreme end"],["para-amṛtāḥ","m. nom. pl.","(being) supremely immortal"],["parimucyanti sarve","pres. 3 pl. + nom. pl.","are all fully released"]],"lit":"\"Strivers, their goal well ascertained by the knowledge of Vedānta, purified in being through the yoga of renunciation — they all, supremely immortal, are wholly released in the Brahma-worlds at the time of the supreme end.\"","para":"Quoted from <strong>Muṇḍaka 3.2.6</strong> (with <em>brahmalokeṣu</em> for Muṇḍaka's <em>brahmalokāḥ</em>), this verse supplies the <strong>curriculum</strong> behind verse 3's <em>tyāga</em>. Four stages are named in order: (1) <strong>vedānta-vijñāna</strong> — not opinion but <em>vijñāna</em>, discriminative knowledge of the Upaniṣadic teaching; (2) <strong>suniścita-artha</strong> — the meaning <strong>settled beyond wavering</strong> (this is the work of <em>manana</em>, reflection); (3) <strong>saṃnyāsa-yoga</strong> — renunciation practised as a discipline; (4) <strong>śuddha-sattva</strong> — the resulting transparency of mind, in which knowledge can finally be <em>lived</em>. The verse also preserves the classical doctrine of <strong>krama-mukti</strong>, gradual liberation: those who do not attain here attain <strong>at the \"supreme end\"</strong> (<em>parāntakāla</em> — read as the moment of death, or as the dissolution of the Brahma-world at the end of the cosmic cycle), being released <em>along with</em> Hiraṇyagarbha.","coms":[["UBY","<em>parāntakāla</em> = the end of the body for the <em>jīvanmukta</em>; for others, the dissolution of Brahmaloka, whence there is no return (<em>krama-mukti</em>)."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>śuddha-sattva</em> is the <em>pramāṇa</em>-condition: the Upaniṣadic sentence cannot produce immediate knowledge in an impure mind, exactly as a clean mirror is needed for a clear image."],["Chinmayānanda","the four terms map the whole path — <em>śravaṇa</em> (vedānta-vijñāna), <em>manana</em> (suniścitārtha), <em>nididhyāsana</em> + detachment (saṃnyāsa-yoga), and the fruit (śuddha-sattva)."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","purity of <em>sattva</em> is the practical crux; all the sādhanā is for this one thing, since the Self is never absent, only unnoticed by a turbid mind."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","the borrowing from Muṇḍaka is exact enough to date Kaivalya after it; the plural <em>brahmalokeṣu</em> is the weaker reading and probably a transmission variant."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 5","title":"The seat: where and how to sit","sa":["विविक्तदेशे च सुखासनस्थः शुचिः समग्रीवशिरःशरीरः ।","अत्याश्रमस्थः सकलेन्द्रियाणि निरुध्य भक्त्या स्वगुरुं प्रणम्य ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["vivikta-deśe ca sukhāsana-sthaḥ śuciḥ samagrīva-śiraḥ-śarīraḥ |","atyāśrama-sthaḥ sakalendriyāṇi nirudhya bhaktyā svaguruṃ praṇamya || 5 ||"],"words":[["vivikta-deśe ca","m. loc. sg.","and in a solitary (secluded) place"],["sukha-āsana-sthaḥ","m. nom. sg.","seated in a comfortable posture"],["śuciḥ","m. nom. sg.","pure (clean, in body and mind)"],["sama-grīvā-śiraḥ-śarīraḥ","bahuvrīhi, m. nom.","with neck, head and body held even (erect and aligned)"],["atyāśrama-sthaḥ","m. nom. sg.","established in the state beyond the āśramas (the highest renunciate)"],["sakala-indriyāṇi nirudhya","n. acc. pl. + abs. (ni-√rudh)","having restrained all the senses"],["bhaktyā svaguruṃ praṇamya","f. instr. + m. acc. + abs.","having bowed to his own guru with devotion"]],"lit":"\"And in a solitary place, seated in an easy posture, pure, with body, neck and head held even, established beyond the āśramas, having restrained all the senses, having bowed with devotion to his own teacher —\"","para":"The verse is a single long <strong>participial run-up</strong> — it has no main verb, because its main verb is in verse 7 (<em>dhyātvā... gacchati</em>). Grammatically and spiritually, everything from 5 to 7 is <strong>one act</strong>. Seven conditions are named, and they are the standard yogic set, here compressed with unusual economy: <strong>solitude</strong> (<em>vivikta-deśa</em> — outwardly a quiet place, inwardly a mind \"separated\" from crowding), <strong>a posture that is easy</strong> (<em>sukha-āsana</em> — not an athletic feat; what can be <em>sustained</em> without the body clamouring), <strong>cleanliness</strong>, <strong>the erect axis</strong> (head, neck and trunk <em>sama</em> — the single most repeated physical instruction in the Upaniṣads, cf. Śvet. 2.8, Gītā 6.13), <strong>the station beyond the āśramas</strong>, <strong>sense-restraint</strong>, and — last and climactic — <strong>salutation to one's own guru with devotion</strong>. That <em>bhakti</em> appears here, in the middle of a posture description, is characteristic: for this text the teacher is not preliminary to the practice, he is part of the seat.","coms":[["UBY","<em>atyāśramin</em> = the <em>paramahaṃsa</em>, who stands beyond the four <em>āśramas</em> because he has no further duty to perform; cf. Śvetāśvatara 6.21, where the teaching is given to <em>atyāśramins</em>."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>śuci</em> covers both <em>bāhya</em> and <em>ābhyantara</em> purity; <em>vivikta</em> is glossed twice — a lonely place, and discrimination (<em>viveka</em>) itself."],["Chinmayānanda","\"comfortable\" is the operative word — <em>sukhāsana</em>, not <em>duḥkhāsana</em>; a posture that must be endured becomes the object of meditation instead of its support. The erect spine is for alertness, not asceticism."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","the verse is a complete set of instructions for the seat: place, posture, purity, alignment, detachment, restraint, and surrender to the guru — omit the last and the rest becomes technique without grace."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","the variant <em>antyāśrama-sthaḥ</em> (\"established in the last āśrama,\" i.e. saṃnyāsa) is well attested and gives the same sense with less strength; <em>atyāśrama</em> is the harder and probably original reading."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 6","title":"The place of meditation: the lotus of the heart","sa":["हृत्पुण्डरीकं विरजं विशुद्धं विचिन्त्य मध्ये विशदं विशोकम् ।","अचिन्त्यमव्यक्तमनन्तरूपं शिवं प्रशान्तममृतं ब्रह्मयोनिम् ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["hṛt-puṇḍarīkaṃ virajaṃ viśuddhaṃ vicintya madhye viśadaṃ viśokam |","acintyam avyaktam ananta-rūpaṃ śivaṃ praśāntam amṛtaṃ brahma-yonim || 6 ||"],"words":[["hṛt-puṇḍarīkaṃ","n. acc. sg.","the lotus of the heart"],["virajaṃ viśuddham","n. acc.","dust-free, utterly pure"],["vicintya","abs. (vi-√cint)","having contemplated (visualised)"],["madhye viśadaṃ viśokam","loc. + n. acc. ×2","in the middle (of it), the bright, the sorrowless"],["acintyam avyaktam","n. acc.","the unthinkable, the unmanifest"],["ananta-rūpam","n. acc.","of endless forms"],["śivaṃ praśāntam amṛtam","n. acc. ×3","the auspicious, the utterly tranquil, the immortal"],["brahma-yonim","m./n. acc.","the source (womb) of Brahmā / the source that is Brahman"]],"lit":"\"Having contemplated the lotus of the heart — dust-free, utterly pure — (and) in its middle the bright, the sorrowless, the unthinkable, the unmanifest, of endless forms, auspicious, utterly tranquil, immortal, the womb of Brahmā —\"","para":"The <strong>place</strong> is given before the <strong>object</strong>, and the place is not anatomical but contemplative: the <strong><em>hṛt-puṇḍarīka</em></strong>, the lotus of the heart of the <em>dahara-vidyā</em> (Chāndogya 8.1), here qualified by <em>viraja</em> and <em>viśuddha</em> — the meditator is to <strong>imagine it already clean</strong>, because the seat of the vision must be made fit before the vision is sought. Then, in its middle, nine words for the Deity, and they are deliberately <strong>contradictory</strong>: it is <strong>bright</strong> (<em>viśada</em>) yet <strong>unmanifest</strong> (<em>avyakta</em>); it is to be <strong>contemplated</strong> (<em>vicintya</em>) yet it is <strong>unthinkable</strong> (<em>acintya</em>); it is <strong>formless-beyond</strong> yet <strong>of endless forms</strong> (<em>ananta-rūpa</em>). This is the Upaniṣad teaching the meditator how to hold the object: as an <em>image that keeps dissolving</em>. And the last word turns the key — <strong><em>brahma-yoni</em></strong>, the womb from which even the creator comes: the God in your heart is the source of the god you are asking (Brahmā is the speaker!).","coms":[["UBY","<em>brahma-yoni</em> is read both ways — the origin <em>of</em> Brahmā (Hiraṇyagarbha) and the origin <em>which is</em> Brahman; the epithet forestalls any notion that the heart-deity is a lesser form."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>viraja/viśuddha</em> are prescriptive, not descriptive: the <em>cleansing</em> of the heart-lotus by <em>sattva</em> is itself part of the meditation. <em>Viśoka</em> recalls Chāndogya's daharākāśa \"in which are contained all desires, free from evil, free from old age and sorrow.\""],["Chinmayānanda","the \"endless forms\" and \"unthinkable\" together mean: use a form, but hold it lightly — the form is a doorway the meditator must be willing to walk through and leave behind."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","practically — first steady the mind <em>on the heart-region</em>, purify by <em>japa</em> and prayer until the space there feels clean and bright, then install the Lord in that space. Do not attempt the formless first."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","a compressed reuse of the <em>dahara</em> teaching of Chāndogya 8.1 and Taittirīya 1.6, with the Śaiva epithet <em>śiva</em> (which in older texts is still an adjective, \"auspicious\") standing at the pivot between attribute and name — exactly the transition this Upaniṣad documents."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 7","title":"The object of meditation: Umā's consort, the three-eyed Lord","sa":["तमादिमध्यान्तविहीनमेकं विभुं चिदानन्दमरूपमद्भुतम् ।","उमासहायं परमेश्वरं प्रभुं त्रिलोचनं नीलकण्ठं प्रशान्तम् ।","ध्यात्वा मुनिर्गच्छति भूतयोनिं समस्तसाक्षिं तमसः परस्तात् ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["tam ādi-madhyānta-vihīnam ekaṃ vibhuṃ cidānandam arūpam adbhutam |","umā-sahāyaṃ parameśvaraṃ prabhuṃ trilocanaṃ nīlakaṇṭhaṃ praśāntam |","dhyātvā munir gacchati bhūta-yoniṃ samasta-sākṣiṃ tamasaḥ parastāt || 7 ||"],"words":[["tam ādi-madhya-anta-vihīnam","m. acc. sg. + bahuvrīhi","Him who is devoid of beginning, middle and end"],["ekaṃ vibhum","m. acc.","the One, the all-pervading"],["cid-ānandam arūpam adbhutam","m./n. acc.","consciousness-bliss, formless, wondrous"],["umā-sahāyam","m. acc.","whose companion is Umā"],["parama-īśvaraṃ prabhum","m. acc. ×2","the Supreme Lord, the Master"],["tri-locanaṃ nīla-kaṇṭhaṃ praśāntam","m. acc. ×3","three-eyed, blue-throated, utterly tranquil"],["dhyātvā muniḥ gacchati","abs. + m. nom. + pres. 3 sg.","having meditated, the sage goes to"],["bhūta-yonim","m. acc.","the source of (all) beings"],["samasta-sākṣim","m. acc.","the Witness of everything"],["tamasaḥ parastāt","n. abl./gen. + adv.","beyond the darkness"]],"lit":"\"Having meditated on Him — devoid of beginning, middle and end; the One, all-pervading; consciousness-and-bliss; formless, wondrous; whose companion is Umā, the supreme Lord, the master, three-eyed, blue-throated, utterly tranquil — the sage goes to the source of beings, the witness of all, beyond darkness.\"","para":"The <strong>main verb arrives</strong> (<em>gacchati</em>), completing the sentence begun in verse 5: <em>seated... having bowed... having contemplated the heart... having meditated on Him — <strong>the sage goes<strong><em>. And the object of meditation is the Upaniṣad's boldest stroke: in one breath the </strong>metaphysical absolute<strong> (beginningless, one, all-pervading, </em>cid-ānanda<em>, formless) and the </strong>iconographic Śiva of the tradition<strong> (with </strong>Umā<strong> beside Him, </strong>three-eyed<strong>, </strong>blue-throated<strong> from the poison He swallowed for the world). Neither is subordinated to the other. This is not the Absolute \"symbolised by\" Śiva, nor Śiva \"explained away as\" the Absolute: it is the claim that the </strong>personal God with His Śakti simply is<strong> the impersonal </em>cidānanda<em>. The destination is described in three terms that will govern the rest of the text: </strong>bhūta-yoni<strong> (source of all beings), </em></strong>samasta-sākṣin<strong><em> (the Witness of everything — the word that returns at v. 18 and v. 23 and becomes the practice), and </em></strong>tamasaḥ parastāt</strong></em> (beyond darkness — beyond <em>avidyā</em>, and beyond the unmanifest <em>prakṛti</em> which the Sāṃkhya calls <em>tamas</em>).","coms":[["UBY","<em>umā-sahāya</em> is glossed as the Lord united with <em>māyā-śakti</em> — not two, since Umā is His own power (cf. <em>devātma-śakti</em>, Śvet. 1.3); <em>trilocana</em> = the three eyes as sun, moon and fire, i.e. the three times and three <em>pramāṇas</em> surveyed at once."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>tamasaḥ parastāt</em> is quoted from the Puruṣa-sūkta tradition (<em>āditya-varṇaṃ tamasaḥ parastāt</em>); the meditator \"goes to\" what he already is — <em>gacchati</em> means <em>prāpnoti</em> in the sense of <em>aprāpta-prāpti</em>, the attainment of the never-unattained."],["Chinmayānanda","the two halves of the verse are the two halves of the meditator: the intellect needs <em>arūpa</em> and <em>ādi-madhyānta-vihīna</em>, the heart needs <em>Umā-sahāya</em> and <em>nīlakaṇṭha</em>. Give each what it needs, and the mind becomes single."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","meditate on the form with love until the form becomes transparent; the <em>samasta-sākṣī</em> is then found not in front of you but behind you, as the one who was looking."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","the verse is decisive evidence for the text's <strong>Śaiva</strong> affiliation and for the maturity of the Umā-Maheśvara iconography at its date; <em>nīlakaṇṭha</em> presupposes the churning-of-the-ocean myth."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 8–9","title":"He is every god, and knowing Him one crosses death","sa":["स ब्रह्मा स शिवः सेन्द्रः सोऽक्षरः परमः स्वराट् ।","स एव विष्णुः स प्राणः स कालोऽग्निः स चन्द्रमाः ॥ ८ ॥","स एव सर्वं यद्भूतं यच्च भव्यं सनातनम् ।","ज्ञात्वा तं मृत्युमत्येति नान्यः पन्था विमुक्तये ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["sa brahmā sa śivaḥ sendraḥ so 'kṣaraḥ paramaḥ svarāṭ |","sa eva viṣṇuḥ sa prāṇaḥ sa kālo 'gniḥ sa candramāḥ || 8 ||","sa eva sarvaṃ yad bhūtaṃ yac ca bhavyaṃ sanātanam |","jñātvā taṃ mṛtyum atyeti nānyaḥ panthā vimuktaye || 9 ||"],"words":[["sa brahmā sa śivaḥ sa indraḥ","m. nom. sg.","He is Brahmā, He is Śiva, He is Indra"],["saḥ akṣaraḥ paramaḥ svarāṭ","m. nom.","He is the Imperishable, the Supreme, the Self-ruler"],["sa eva viṣṇuḥ sa prāṇaḥ","m. nom.","He alone is Viṣṇu, He is the life-breath"],["sa kālaḥ agniḥ sa candramāḥ","m. nom.","He is Time, (He is) Fire, He is the Moon"],["sa eva sarvaṃ yat bhūtam","n. nom. + rel. + pp.","He alone is all that has been"],["yat ca bhavyaṃ sanātanam","rel. + fut. part. + n.","and what is to be — the everlasting"],["jñātvā tam","abs. + m. acc.","having known Him"],["mṛtyum atyeti","m. acc. + pres. (ati-√i)","one crosses beyond death"],["na anyaḥ panthā vimuktaye","nom. + m. nom. + f. dat.","there is no other path to release"]],"lit":"\"He is Brahmā, He is Śiva, He is Indra; He is the Imperishable, the Supreme, the Self-Resplendent. He alone is Viṣṇu, He is prāṇa, He is Time, Fire, the Moon. He alone is all that has been and all that shall be, the eternal. Knowing Him one crosses death; there is no other path to release.\"","para":"Having installed <strong>Śiva</strong> as the object of meditation, the text immediately <strong>refuses sectarianism</strong> — and does so by the strongest available means: it puts <em>śiva</em> in a list, alongside Brahmā, Indra and <strong>Viṣṇu</strong>, and says the One <strong>is all of them</strong>. Then it widens past the gods to the <strong>cosmic functions</strong> — <em>prāṇa</em> (life), <em>kāla</em> (time), <em>agni</em> and <em>candramas</em> (the fire and moon that measure the sacrifice and the month) — and past those to <strong>all that has been and will be</strong> (a verbatim echo of Puruṣa-sūkta <em>puruṣa evedaṃ sarvaṃ yad bhūtaṃ yac ca bhavyam</em>). Verse 9 then draws the practical conclusion in the idiom of <strong>Śvetāśvatara 3.8</strong>: <em>jñātvā</em> — by <strong>knowing</strong>, not by propitiating — <strong>one crosses death</strong>, and — <em>nānyaḥ panthā</em> — there is <strong>no other road</strong>. The \"no other path\" is not a threat but a definition: since bondage is ignorance of what one is, nothing but knowledge can be its remedy.","coms":[["UBY","the list is <em>aikya-pratipādana</em>, the establishing of oneness: each name is a <em>upādhi</em> (limiting form) of the one <em>caitanya</em>; <em>svarāṭ</em> (\"self-ruling\") is the key word, marking Him as dependent on nothing."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>akṣara</em> and <em>parama</em> distinguish the two Brahmans of Muṇḍaka; <em>sa eva</em> repeated is the sentence's whole force — \"He <strong>alone</strong>,\" to exclude any second principle."],["Chinmayānanda","the verse cures the disease of sectarian religion at the root: to fight over the name is to have missed what the names name. The gods are functions; He is the reality functioning."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","in worship, take your chosen form (<em>iṣṭa-devatā</em>) and know it to be the All; then devotion and non-dual knowledge cease to be rivals."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","an explicit early statement of the <em>ekadeva</em> / <em>pan-theistic identification</em> formula familiar from the Atharvan Upaniṣads (cf. Atharvaśiras); 9b is quoted from Śvetāśvatara 3.8 with <em>vimuktaye</em> replacing <em>vidyate 'yanāya</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 10","title":"The Self in all beings, all beings in the Self","sa":["सर्वभूतस्थमात्मानं सर्वभूतानि चात्मनि ।","सम्पश्यन्ब्रह्म परमं याति नान्येन हेतुना ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["sarva-bhūta-stham ātmānaṃ sarva-bhūtāni cātmani |","sampaśyan brahma paramaṃ yāti nānyena hetunā || 10 ||"],"words":[["sarva-bhūta-stham ātmānam","m. acc. sg.","the Self as abiding in all beings"],["sarva-bhūtāni ca ātmani","n. acc. pl. + m. loc.","and all beings in the Self"],["sampaśyan","pres. part. m. nom.","(fully) seeing"],["brahma paramaṃ yāti","n. acc. + pres.","he attains the supreme Brahman"],["na anyena hetunā","instr. sg.","not by any other means"]],"lit":"\"Seeing the Self as abiding in all beings, and all beings in the Self, one attains the supreme Brahman — not by any other means.\"","para":"The <strong>double vision</strong>, stated in the form it takes in Īśa 6 and Gītā 6.29: not merely \"God is in everything\" (which leaves the many intact and adds a divine ingredient), nor merely \"everything is in God\" (which can remain a pious cosmology), but <strong>both at once</strong> — <em>sarvabhūtastham ātmānam</em> <strong>and</strong> <em>sarvabhūtāni ātmani</em>. Held together, the two clauses cancel the very distinction of container and contained, which is precisely the point. The participle is <strong><em>sam-paśyan</em></strong> — present, continuous, <em>seeing</em>, not \"having concluded.\" And the closing half-line repeats verse 9's refusal: <strong>not by any other means.</strong> Verses 9 and 10 thus give the two faces of the single knowledge — <strong>vertical</strong> (He is all the gods and all time) and <strong>horizontal</strong> (He is all beings and I am in Him).","coms":[["UBY","the two clauses correspond to <em>vyaṣṭi</em> and <em>samaṣṭi</em> — the Self in each individual, and all individuals in the total Self; realising their identity is <em>brahma-prāpti</em>."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>sampaśyan</em> is <em>aparokṣa-darśana</em>, immediate seeing, not inference; hence \"no other means\" — even scripture only points, it cannot see for you."],["Chinmayānanda","this is the ethical consequence of the metaphysics: one who actually sees this cannot injure, because there is no \"other\" left to injure. Vedānta's ethics is not a rule added to its truth; it is that truth behaving."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","practise it as a daily discipline — in every meeting, look for the one Self on both sides of the encounter. This is <em>bhūta-dayā</em> grounded in <em>advaita</em>."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","nearly identical to Īśa 6 and reused at Gītā 6.29; one of the most widely transmitted Upaniṣadic verses, here inserted to universalise the Śaiva meditation just given."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 11","title":"The two fire-sticks: Self and OM","sa":["आत्मानमरणिं कृत्वा प्रणवं चोत्तरारणिम् ।","ज्ञाननिर्मथनाभ्यासात्पापं दहति पण्डितः ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["ātmānam araṇiṃ kṛtvā praṇavaṃ cottarāraṇim |","jñāna-nirmathanābhyāsāt pāpaṃ dahati paṇḍitaḥ || 11 ||"],"words":[["ātmānam araṇiṃ kṛtvā","m. acc. + f. acc. + abs.","having made the self (body) the lower fire-stick"],["praṇavaṃ ca uttara-araṇim","m. acc. + f. acc.","and the praṇava (OM) the upper fire-stick"],["jñāna-nirmathana-abhyāsāt","m. abl. sg.","by the practice of churning that is knowledge"],["pāpaṃ dahati paṇḍitaḥ","n. acc. + pres. + m. nom.","the wise man burns up evil"]],"lit":"\"Having made the self the lower fire-stick and OM the upper fire-stick, by the repeated churning that is knowledge, the wise man burns up evil.\"","para":"The <strong>araṇi</strong> image, taken from <strong>Śvetāśvatara 1.14</strong>, is the Upaniṣadic picture of meditation as <strong>friction</strong>. Two sticks, one held steady below, one turned above: fire is not <em>brought</em> to the wood — it is <strong>already latent there</strong> and is <em>drawn out</em> by patient rubbing. So here: the <strong>self/body</strong> is the fixed base, <strong><em>OM</em></strong> the turning drill, and <strong><em>abhyāsa</em></strong> — repetition, the unglamorous word at the centre of the verse — the friction. Note <em>nirmathana</em>: churning, with the sense of thorough extraction (the same verb used of churning the ocean). What burns is <strong>pāpa</strong> — and a well-attested variant reads <strong><em>pāśam</em></strong>, \"the noose/bond,\" which suits the Śaiva vocabulary of <em>paśu–pāśa–pati</em> exactly and may be the older reading. Either way, the fire consumes what binds: sin understood as the knot, not as a ledger entry.","coms":[["UBY","<em>ātmā</em> here = the <em>jīva</em> or the body as the <em>adhāra</em>; <em>jñāna</em> is not the result of the churning but its very act — knowing <em>is</em> the friction, hence <em>jñāna-nirmathana</em>."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the <em>praṇava</em> is the upper <em>araṇi</em> because it is the <em>pratīka</em>, the symbol by which the unmanifest is handled; sustained <em>praṇava</em>-meditation rubs away the <em>adhyāsa</em> (superimposition)."],["Chinmayānanda","<em>abhyāsa</em> is the word to underline — the sticks must be turned <em>continuously</em>; intermittent rubbing never kindles fire, and intermittent meditation never kindles knowledge. Heat lost between sittings must be regained."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","the practical instruction: sit, take OM, and let it turn steadily in the field of the body-mind until warmth (concentration) becomes flame (absorption). <em>Pāśa</em>-reading preferred in the Śaiva lineages."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","a direct citation of Śvet. 1.14 (<em>sva-deham araṇiṃ kṛtvā praṇavaṃ cottarāraṇim</em>) with <em>ātmānam</em> for <em>sva-deham</em>; the variant <em>pāśam</em> is recorded in the e-text and in several printed editions."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 12–13","title":"The one who wakes, dreams, and sleeps","sa":["स एव मायापरिमोहितात्मा शरीरमास्थाय करोति सर्वम् ।","स्त्र्यन्नपानादिविचित्रभोगैः स एव जाग्रत्परितृप्तिमेति ॥ १२ ॥","स्वप्ने स जीवः सुखदुःखभोक्ता स्वमायया कल्पितजीवलोके ।","सुषुप्तिकाले सकले विलीने तमोऽभिभूतः सुखरूपमेति ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["sa eva māyā-parimohitātmā śarīram āsthāya karoti sarvam |","stryanna-pānādi-vicitra-bhogaiḥ sa eva jāgrat paritṛptim eti || 12 ||","svapne sa jīvaḥ sukha-duḥkha-bhoktā svamāyayā kalpita-jīvaloke |","suṣupti-kāle sakale vilīne tamo 'bhibhūtaḥ sukha-rūpam eti || 13 ||"],"words":[["sa eva māyā-parimohita-ātmā","m. nom. + bahuvrīhi","He alone, His self utterly deluded by māyā"],["śarīram āsthāya karoti sarvam","n. acc. + abs. + pres. + acc.","taking up a body, does everything"],["strī-anna-pāna-ādi-vicitra-bhogaiḥ","m. instr. pl.","by the varied enjoyments — woman, food, drink and the rest"],["sa eva jāgrat paritṛptim eti","nom. + pres. part. (waking) + f. acc. + pres.","He alone, waking, attains satisfaction"],["svapne sa jīvaḥ","m. loc. + nom.","in dream, that same jīva"],["sukha-duḥkha-bhoktā","m. nom. agent noun","is the experiencer of pleasure and pain"],["sva-māyayā kalpita-jīva-loke","f. instr. + m. loc. pp.","in a world of living beings fashioned by his own māyā"],["suṣupti-kāle sakale vilīne","m. loc. + n. loc. abs.","at the time of deep sleep, when all has dissolved"],["tamaḥ abhibhūtaḥ","n. + pp. m. nom.","overcome by darkness (tamas)"],["sukha-rūpam eti","n. acc. + pres.","he attains the form of happiness"]],"lit":"\"He alone, His very self bewildered by māyā, taking up a body, does everything; by the varied enjoyments — woman, food, drink and the rest — He alone, in waking, attains satisfaction. In dream, that same <em>jīva</em> is the experiencer of pleasure and pain in a world of beings fashioned by his own māyā. In deep sleep, when everything has dissolved, overcome by darkness, he becomes of the nature of happiness.\"","para":"The Upaniṣad's <strong>avasthā-traya</strong> analysis, and it opens with a shock: <strong><em>sa eva</em></strong> — \"<strong>He alone</strong>,\" the same Parameśvara of verses 7–9 — is the one who, \"bewildered by māyā,\" takes a body and goes about the business of living. The God being meditated upon and the person doing the meditating are <strong>one subject under two conditions</strong>. Then the three states are laid out with their distinguishing marks: in <strong>waking</strong>, enjoyment of <em>gross</em> objects (the list is frank — <em>strī, anna, pāna</em> — and the point is not condemnation but classification: these are what <em>jāgrat</em> has to offer, and the word is <em>paritṛpti</em>, satisfaction, which by its nature passes); in <strong>dream</strong>, experience of pleasure and pain in a world <strong>made of oneself</strong> (<em>sva-māyayā kalpita</em> — the decisive phrase, since in dream the maker, the world and the experiencer are demonstrably one substance); in <strong>deep sleep</strong>, everything <strong>dissolved</strong> (<em>sakale vilīne</em>), and a happiness that is real but <strong>covered by tamas</strong> — <em>sukha-rūpam eti</em>, he <em>becomes</em> of the form of happiness, but as one overcome, not as one knowing. Deep sleep is thus a <strong>preview of the Self with the lights off</strong>: the bliss is genuine, the knowledge is absent — which is exactly what verses 17–18 will correct.","coms":[["UBY","the three states are the three <em>upādhis</em> — <em>viśva</em>, <em>taijasa</em>, <em>prājña</em> (Māṇḍūkya 3–5). <em>Tamo 'bhibhūtaḥ</em> marks the difference between <em>suṣupti</em> and <em>turīya</em>: the same absence of duality, with and without awareness."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>sva-māyayā kalpita</em> in dream is the <em>dṛṣṭānta</em> (illustration) for waking too — the argument the Gauḍapāda-kārikās develop at length; the Upaniṣad states it and moves on."],["Chinmayānanda","watch the pronoun. One <em>jīva</em> travels all three states and reports on them afterwards — \"I slept well.\" The reporter is not any of the three; find <em>him</em>, and the whole Upaniṣad is finished."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","the practical use is the nightly review: before sleep, recollect that the one who is about to lose the world is the same one who made it appear at dawn. Deep sleep, entered consciously, becomes <em>samādhi</em>."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","the standard post-Māṇḍūkya state-analysis, notable here for the blunt <em>sa eva</em> identifying the transmigrant with the Lord — a strong Advaita formulation embedded in a Śaiva text."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 14","title":"The three cities, and That in which they dissolve","sa":["पुनश्च जन्मान्तरकर्मयोगात्स एव जीवः स्वपिति प्रबुद्धः ।","पुरत्रये क्रीडति यश्च जीवस्ततस्तु जातं सकलं विचित्रम् ।","आधारमानन्दमखण्डबोधं यस्मिँल्लयं याति पुरत्रयं च ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["punaś ca janmāntara-karma-yogāt sa eva jīvaḥ svapiti prabuddhaḥ |","puratraye krīḍati yaś ca jīvas tatas tu jātaṃ sakalaṃ vicitram |","ādhāram ānandam akhaṇḍa-bodhaṃ yasmiṃl layaṃ yāti puratrayaṃ ca || 14 ||"],"words":[["punaḥ ca janma-antara-karma-yogāt","adv. + m. abl. sg.","and again, through connection with the karma of other births"],["sa eva jīvaḥ svapiti prabuddhaḥ","nom. + pres. + pp. nom.","that same jīva sleeps and wakes"],["pura-traye krīḍati yaḥ ca jīvaḥ","n. loc. + pres. + rel. nom.","and the jīva who sports in the three cities"],["tataḥ tu jātaṃ sakalaṃ vicitram","abl. + pp. n. nom.","from That indeed has arisen all this manifold"],["ādhāram ānandam akhaṇḍa-bodham","n. acc. ×3","(That which is) the support, bliss, undivided awareness"],["yasmin layaṃ yāti puratrayaṃ ca","loc. rel. + m. acc. + pres. + n. nom.","and in which the three cities go to dissolution"]],"lit":"\"And again, through the karma-link of other births, that same <em>jīva</em> sleeps and wakes. And the <em>jīva</em> who plays in the three cities — from That indeed has all this manifold arisen: the support, bliss, undivided awareness, in which the three cities also dissolve.\"","para":"The cycle <strong>closes and repeats</strong>: <em>karma</em> from former births turns the wheel of waking and sleeping again, life after life. The image for the three states is now <strong><em>puratraya<em>, the three cities<strong> — a phrase with a double charge: the three states/bodies as walled towns the </em>jīva<em> moves between, and behind it the myth of </strong>Tripurāntaka<strong>, Śiva the destroyer of the three cities. The </em>jīva<em> </em></strong>krīḍati<strong><em> — \"plays, sports\" — in them; the word is not accidental, for from the standpoint the verse is about to take, the wandering is </strong>play<strong>, not tragedy. Then the pivot: </em>tatas tu jātaṃ sakalaṃ vicitram<em> — </strong>\"from That has all this variety arisen\"<strong> — and three words for That: </em></strong>ādhāra<strong><em> (the support on which it all rests), </em></strong>ānanda<strong><em> (the bliss glimpsed in deep sleep, now named as the substance), and </em></strong>akhaṇḍa-bodha</strong></em> (<strong>undivided awareness</strong> — the phrase that answers verse 13's <em>tamo 'bhibhūtaḥ</em>: the same bliss, no longer in the dark). And in That the three cities <strong>dissolve</strong>. Origin, support, and dissolution of all three states — the Upaniṣadic definition of Brahman (<em>janmādy asya yataḥ</em>) applied to the inner world.","coms":[["UBY","<em>puratraya</em> = the three bodies (<em>sthūla, sūkṣma, kāraṇa</em>) which are the \"cities\" of the three states; <em>akhaṇḍa-bodha</em> is the technical Advaita term for the <em>nirvikalpa</em> consciousness that is not divided by knower, knowing and known."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>ādhāra</em> answers \"on what does experience rest?\", <em>ānanda</em> \"why is the Self desirable?\", <em>akhaṇḍa-bodha</em> \"what is its nature?\" — the three make a complete definition, anticipating <em>satyaṃ jñānam anantam</em>."],["Chinmayānanda","the <em>jīva</em> \"plays\" — it is in fact a dreadful play, but the verse's lightness is deliberate: seen from the support, the whole traffic between the three cities has the weight of a game."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","the meditation prescribed here is <em>laya-cintana</em>: watch waking dissolve into dream, dream into sleep, and hold to what does not dissolve."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","<em>puratraya</em> joins the Upaniṣadic \"nine-gated city\" (Śvet. 3.18) imagery to the Śaiva Tripura myth; the verse is metrically irregular (three <em>pādas</em>-pairs) and may combine two originally separate units."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 15","title":"The emanation of the world","sa":["एतस्माज्जायते प्राणो मनः सर्वेन्द्रियाणि च ।","खं वायुर्ज्योतिरापश्च पृथ्वी विश्वस्य धारिणी ॥ १५ ॥"],"iast":["etasmāj jāyate prāṇo manaḥ sarvendriyāṇi ca |","khaṃ vāyur jyotir āpaś ca pṛthvī viśvasya dhāriṇī || 15 ||"],"words":[["etasmāt jāyate","m. abl. sg. + pres.","from This is born"],["prāṇaḥ manaḥ sarva-indriyāṇi ca","m./n. nom.","the life-breath, mind, and all the senses"],["kham vāyuḥ jyotiḥ āpaḥ ca","n./m./n./f. nom.","space, air, fire (light), and the waters"],["pṛthvī viśvasya dhāriṇī","f. nom. + gen. + f. nom.","(and) earth, the supporter of all"]],"lit":"\"From This are born the life-breath, the mind, and all the senses; space, air, fire, the waters, and earth, the supporter of all.\"","para":"A verbatim borrowing of the cosmogony of <strong>Muṇḍaka 2.1.3</strong>. Two things are worth noticing. First, the <strong>order</strong>: <em>prāṇa</em>, <em>manas</em> and the <strong>senses come first</strong>, the five <strong>elements after</strong> — the Upaniṣadic sequence puts the subjective apparatus before the objective world, because the world is being derived as <em>the world of an experiencer</em>. Second, the <strong>ablative</strong> <em>etasmāt</em>: \"from <strong>This</strong>\" — with the near-demonstrative, pointing back to the <em>ādhāra-ānanda-akhaṇḍa-bodha</em> of verse 14, i.e. <strong>to the Self that was just found by looking inward</strong>, not to a remote first cause. The verse thus performs the classical Vedāntic reversal: having traced the three states back to their ground, the text now derives the entire cosmos <em>forward</em> from that same ground — so that the outer world and the inner witness are shown to have one address. The last epithet, <em>viśvasya dhāriṇī</em> (\"earth, supporter of all\"), quietly rhymes with <em>ādhāram</em> in v. 14: earth supports the world; awareness supports the earth.","coms":[["UBY","<em>etasmāt</em> = from the <em>akhaṇḍa-bodha</em> just named; the emanation is <em>vivarta</em> (apparent transformation), not a real production, as vv. 19 and 23 will make explicit."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the sequence follows Muṇḍaka and Taittirīya (<em>ākāśād vāyuḥ...</em>); <em>jyotis</em> = <em>tejas</em>/fire; the inclusion of <em>prāṇa</em> and <em>manas</em> before the elements marks the subtle before the gross."],["Chinmayānanda","the verse answers the objection \"if all is the Self, whence the world?\" — the world is not <em>besides</em> That, it is <em>from</em> That, as a dream is from the dreamer, and no dream ever diminished a sleeper."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","run the ladder in reverse as a practice: earth → water → fire → air → space → senses → mind → prāṇa → the Self. This is the classical <em>pañcīkaraṇa</em>-based <em>laya</em>."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","a direct citation of Muṇḍaka 2.1.3, confirming the compiler's Muṇḍaka source (already used at v. 4)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 16","title":"\"That art thou; thou art That\"","sa":["यत्परं ब्रह्म सर्वात्मा विश्वस्यायतनं महत् ।","सूक्ष्मात्सूक्ष्मतरं नित्यं तत्त्वमेव त्वमेव तत् ॥ १६ ॥"],"iast":["yat paraṃ brahma sarvātmā viśvasyāyatanaṃ mahat |","sūkṣmāt sūkṣmataraṃ nityaṃ tat tvam eva tvam eva tat || 16 ||"],"words":[["yat paraṃ brahma sarva-ātmā","rel. n. + n. nom. + m. nom.","that which is the supreme Brahman, the Self of all"],["viśvasya āyatanaṃ mahat","gen. + n. nom. + adj.","the great abode (resting-place) of the universe"],["sūkṣmāt sūkṣmataraṃ nityam","abl. + compar. n. + n.","subtler than the subtle, eternal"],["tat tvam eva","pron.","that indeed art thou"],["tvam eva tat","pron.","thou indeed art that"]],"lit":"\"That which is the supreme Brahman, the Self of all, the great abode of the universe, subtler than the subtle, eternal — <strong>that art thou; thou art that.</strong>\"","para":"The <strong>centre of gravity of the Upaniṣad</strong>. The first three <em>pādas</em> pile up the objective description — supreme Brahman, Self of all, the vast <strong>abode</strong> in which the universe rests, <strong>subtler than the subtle</strong>, eternal — building the grandest possible \"That.\" And then the fourth <em>pāda</em> collapses the distance in five syllables, and does it <strong>twice, in both directions</strong>: <strong><em>tat tvam eva</em></strong> — that is <em>you</em> — and <strong><em>tvam eva tat</em></strong> — you are <em>that</em>. The doubling is the whole art of the verse. Said once (as in Chāndogya's <em>tat tvam asi</em>) the sentence can still be heard as a promise of promotion — <em>you will become that</em>. Said in both directions it becomes an <strong>equation</strong>, and an equation has no direction and no future tense. Everything before verse 16 is preparation for it; everything after is its unfolding in the first person.","coms":[["UBY","the <em>mahāvākya</em> of this Upaniṣad. The two halves are <em>jahad-ajahal-lakṣaṇā</em>: the \"thou\" minus its limitations and the \"That\" minus its cosmic attributes meet in the one <em>caitanya</em> — the identity is of the <em>lakṣyārtha</em>, not of the <em>vācyārtha</em>."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the reversal <em>tvam eva tat</em> forestalls the error of a merely devotional reading (\"I belong to That\"); identity, not relation, is asserted."],["after","the meditation (5–7) and"],["after","the analysis of the three states (12–14). The Upaniṣad does not begin with \"you are God\"; it earns it"],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","the practical form is <em>so 'ham</em> / <em>śivo 'ham</em> meditation — not an affirmation repeated at an unconvinced mind, but the conclusion of the preceding enquiry, held steady."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","a deliberate restatement of Chāndogya 6's <em>tat tvam asi</em> in bidirectional form; <em>viśvasyāyatanam</em> echoes Muṇḍaka/Praśna usage of <em>āyatana</em> for the Self as world-abode."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 17","title":"That by which the three states are lit","sa":["जाग्रत्स्वप्नसुषुप्त्यादिप्रपञ्चं यत्प्रकाशते ।","तद्ब्रह्माहमिति ज्ञात्वा सर्वबन्धैः प्रमुच्यते ॥ १७ ॥"],"iast":["jāgrat-svapna-suṣupty-ādi-prapañcaṃ yat prakāśate |","tad brahmāham iti jñātvā sarva-bandhaiḥ pramucyate || 17 ||"],"words":[["jāgrat-svapna-suṣupti-ādi-prapañcam","m. acc. sg.","the expanse (manifold) of waking, dream, deep sleep and the rest"],["yat prakāśate","rel. n. + pres. mid.","that which illumines (shines forth as / reveals)"],["tat brahma aham iti jñātvā","n. + n. + nom. + quot. + abs.","having known \"that Brahman am I\""],["sarva-bandhaiḥ pramucyate","m. instr. pl. + pass. pres.","one is released from all bonds"]],"lit":"\"That which illumines the whole expanse of waking, dream, deep sleep and the rest — having known 'I am that Brahman,' one is released from all bonds.\"","para":"The <strong>argument from illumination</strong>, stated as compactly as it can be stated. Each state cancels the others: waking abolishes dream, dream abolishes waking, sleep abolishes both. Therefore <strong>none of them can be what you are</strong> — but something <strong>shows all three</strong>, since otherwise they could not even be <em>reported</em>. That revealer is <em>prakāśa</em>, self-luminous awareness: it does not need a second light to be seen, and it is never itself one of the things it shows. The little word <strong><em>ādi</em></strong> (\"and the rest\") is doing real work: it leaves the series open — <em>turīya</em>, <em>samādhi</em>, death, whatever further states there may be — and quietly asserts that the same light attends them all. And the knowledge is stated in the <strong>first person</strong> (<em>brahmāham</em>): not \"Brahman exists,\" not \"Brahman illumines this,\" but <strong>I am that.</strong> The result is not the <em>acquisition</em> of freedom but <em>release from bonds</em> — the removal of something that was never real.","coms":[["UBY","<em>prakāśate</em> is used intransitively-causatively: Brahman \"shines,\" and by its shining the manifold is shown (cf. Kaṭha 2.2.15, <em>tasya bhāsā sarvam idaṃ vibhāti</em>)."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the three states are <em>dṛśya</em> (seen), hence <em>jaḍa</em> (insentient); the <em>dṛk</em> (seer) is one and never absent. That is the whole proof."],["Chinmayānanda","consciousness is like the light in a cinema — it shows every scene and is coloured by none. The \"bonds\" are simply the mistake of identifying with the film."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","the practice is <em>sākṣī-bhāva</em> — stand as the one who is aware of the state rather than as the state. Begin with a few minutes; end by living there."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","the <em>prakāśa</em> argument is the common property of the later Advaita Upaniṣads; <em>ādi</em> implicitly acknowledges the Māṇḍūkya fourth."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 18","title":"The Witness: \"I am pure consciousness, I am Sadāśiva\"","sa":["त्रिषु धामसु यद्भोग्यं भोक्ता भोगश्च यद्भवेत् ।","तेभ्यो विलक्षणः साक्षी चिन्मात्रोऽहं सदाशिवः ॥ १८ ॥"],"iast":["triṣu dhāmasu yad bhogyaṃ bhoktā bhogaś ca yad bhavet |","tebhyo vilakṣaṇaḥ sākṣī cinmātro 'haṃ sadāśivaḥ || 18 ||"],"words":[["triṣu dhāmasu","n. loc. pl.","in the three abodes (states)"],["yat bhogyam","rel. + n. nom.","whatever is the enjoyed (object)"],["bhoktā bhogaḥ ca yat bhavet","m. nom. + m. nom. + opt. 3 sg.","and whatever may be the enjoyer and the enjoying"],["tebhyaḥ vilakṣaṇaḥ","m. abl. pl. + m. nom.","distinct (of another character) from them"],["sākṣī cin-mātraḥ aham sadā-śivaḥ","m. nom.","I am the Witness, pure consciousness, the ever-auspicious (Sadāśiva)"]],"lit":"\"Whatever is the enjoyed, the enjoyer and the enjoying in the three abodes — distinct from all these am I, the Witness, mere consciousness, the ever-auspicious.\"","para":"Verse 17 named the light; verse 18 <strong>takes up residence in it</strong>, and the grammar changes permanently — from here to the end, the Upaniṣad speaks as <strong>\"I.\"</strong> The analysis is the classical <em>triputī</em>: every experience in every state decomposes into <strong>object (bhogya), subject (bhoktṛ), and the act of experiencing (bhoga)</strong>. All three are <em>content</em>. What I am is <strong><em>vilakṣaṇa</em></strong> — \"of a different mark\" — from all of them: the <strong><em>sākṣin</em></strong>, witness, <strong><em>cinmātra</em></strong>, <strong>nothing but consciousness</strong> (<em>mātra</em>, \"mere,\" excludes every added attribute), and — the word that names this Upaniṣad's God — <strong><em>Sadāśiva</em></strong>. The verse is the text's exact point of fusion: the Śaiva <strong>Sadāśiva</strong> and the Advaitic <strong>sākṣī-caitanya</strong> are asserted to be one thing, and asserted <em>in the first person by the meditator</em>. The worshipper of verse 7 has become what he worshipped.","coms":[["UBY","<em>sākṣī</em> is the technical <em>kūṭastha-caitanya</em>, unchanging witness-consciousness; <em>cinmātra</em> excludes even <em>sat</em> and <em>ānanda</em> as separate attributes — they are the same one thing described."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>vilakṣaṇa</em> from the <em>triputī</em> is the decisive refutation of any view that makes the Self an experiencer; experiencership belongs to the <em>antaḥkaraṇa</em>, witnessed like everything else."],["Chinmayānanda","the Witness \"does nothing\" — that is not a deficiency but the definition. The moment you find yourself <em>doing</em> the witnessing, you have stepped back into the <em>bhoktṛ</em>; let even that go."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","<em>cinmātro 'haṃ sadāśivaḥ</em> is prescribed as a <em>japa</em>-formula for the advanced practitioner, the Śaiva counterpart of <em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em>."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","the earliest clear Upaniṣadic use of <strong>Sadāśiva</strong> as a name of the Absolute; a landmark for the dating of the Śaiva Upaniṣads and their relation to the emerging Āgamic theology."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 19","title":"\"In me alone all is born\"","sa":["मय्येव सकलं जातं मयि सर्वं प्रतिष्ठितम् ।","मयि सर्वं लयं याति तद्ब्रह्माद्वयमस्म्यहम् ॥ १९ ॥"],"iast":["mayy eva sakalaṃ jātaṃ mayi sarvaṃ pratiṣṭhitam |","mayi sarvaṃ layaṃ yāti tad brahmādvayam asmy aham || 19 ||"],"words":[["mayi eva sakalaṃ jātam","loc. sg. + n. nom. + pp.","in me alone is all born"],["mayi sarvaṃ pratiṣṭhitam","loc. + n. nom. + pp.","in me all is established"],["mayi sarvaṃ layaṃ yāti","loc. + acc. + pres.","in me all goes to dissolution"],["tat brahma advayam asmi aham","n. + n. + n. + pres. 1 sg. + nom.","that non-dual Brahman am I"]],"lit":"\"In me alone is all born; in me is all established; in me all goes to dissolution. That non-dual Brahman am I.\"","para":"The <strong>seal of the first khaṇḍa</strong>, and one of the most quoted verses in Vedānta. The three great cosmic functions — <strong><em>sṛṣṭi, sthiti, laya</em></strong> (creation, maintenance, dissolution), the work of Brahmā, Viṣṇu and Rudra, the business of Īśvara — are here relocated, all three, into the <strong>locative of the first person singular: <em>mayi</em>, \"in me.\"</strong> Note the preposition: not \"<em>by</em> me\" (<em>mayā</em>), which would make the Self a cosmic agent and leave the world real; but \"<strong><em>in<em> me<strong>,\" which makes the world an appearance </em>within<em> awareness — born in it, resting in it, dissolving in it, like the three states of verse 14 in their </em>ādhāra<em>. And the conclusion adds the word that settles the whole matter: </em></strong>advayam</strong></em>, \"<strong>without a second</strong>.\" Not \"I am the greatest being,\" which would still leave others; <strong>non-dual</strong>. This is <em>kaivalya</em> defined — aloneness not as isolation <em>from</em> things, but as there being <strong>nothing else to be isolated from</strong>.","coms":[["UBY","the locative is <em>adhikaraṇa</em>, the substrate: the rope in which the snake is born, rests and dissolves. <em>Advayam</em> rules out both <em>bheda</em> and <em>bhedābheda</em>."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the \"I\" is not the ego (<em>ahaṃkāra</em>) — which is itself born and dissolved in the series — but the <em>pratyagātman</em> revealed as identical with Brahman by v. 16."],["Chinmayānanda","the crucial distinction is <em>mayi</em> versus <em>mayā</em>. The wave-maker is still in the story; the ocean is the story's substance. Vedānta says: you are the ocean."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","a supreme <em>nididhyāsana</em> text — recited slowly, it dissolves the sense of a world standing over against oneself. Best practised at the close of meditation, not the beginning."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","the verse appears, with variations, across the later Advaita and Yoga Upaniṣads and in Purāṇic Vedānta; it is the Kaivalya's most widely transmitted line after v. 24."]],"notes":["॥ प्रथमः खण्डः ॥ १ ॥ — <em>Thus the first khaṇḍa.</em>"]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Khaṇḍa 1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The first khaṇḍa is <strong>one continuous movement in five stages</strong>, and its architecture is unusually exact for a short text:","1. <strong>Qualification (1–5).</strong> Who may receive this? One who <strong>approaches properly</strong> (1), takes <strong>faith, devotion and meditation</strong> as his method (2), has seen through the three Vedic goods of <strong>work, progeny and wealth</strong> (3), has <strong>ascertained the meaning of Vedānta and renounced</strong> (4), and can <strong>sit</strong> — alone, easy, erect, restrained, bowing to his teacher (5). Note that the qualification is <em>four-fifths ethical and one-fifth postural</em>; the text spends more words on who you must be than on how you must sit.","2. <strong>The meditation proper (6–7).</strong> <strong>Place:</strong> the cleaned lotus of the heart. <strong>Object:</strong> a deity described simultaneously as <strong>absolute</strong> (beginningless, one, <em>cidānanda</em>, formless) and <strong>iconic</strong> (Umā's consort, three-eyed, blue-throated). <strong>Result:</strong> <em>the sage goes to the source of beings, the Witness of all, beyond darkness.</em> Everything from v. 5 to v. 7 is grammatically one sentence — the seat, the salutation, the heart and the God are a single act.","3. <strong>The dissolution of difference (8–11).</strong> The moment the God is meditated upon, the text begins removing the walls: He is <strong>every god and every cosmic function</strong> (8–9); He is <strong>the Self in all beings and all beings in the Self</strong> (10); and He is to be <strong>churned out of oneself with OM</strong> (11) — the first clear hint that the object of worship is on the same side of the encounter as the worshipper.","4. <strong>The analysis of experience (12–16).</strong> The proof that this is so comes not from scripture but from <strong>the structure of one's own day</strong>: the same <em>jīva</em> wakes, dreams and sleeps (12–13); plays in the <strong>three cities</strong> and dissolves into their <strong>support, bliss, undivided awareness</strong> (14); from That the whole world proceeds (15); therefore — <strong><em>tat tvam eva tvam eva tat</em></strong> (16). This is the Upaniṣad's central and best-constructed argument: <strong>the ground of the three states and the ground of the cosmos are shown to be the same ground, and then identified with the listener.</strong>","5. <strong>The first person (17–19).</strong> Having earned it, the text switches voice: <em>I am</em> the light in which the states appear (17); <em>I am</em> the Witness, distinct from enjoyer, enjoyed and enjoying — <em>cinmātro 'haṃ sadāśivaḥ</em> (18); <em>in me</em> all is born, rests and dissolves — <em>tad brahmādvayam asmy aham</em> (19).","Two observations worth carrying:","- <strong>Bhakti is not a lower rung here.</strong> Brahmā prescribes <em>bhakti</em> in verse 2, the guru is saluted with <em>bhaktyā</em> in verse 5, and the object of meditation is a God with a consort and a mythology in verse 7. Yet the text arrives at strict non-duality by verse 19 <strong>without ever repudiating any of it</strong>. The transition is managed by identification rather than replacement: <em>cinmātro 'haṃ <strong>sadāśivaḥ</strong></em>.","- <strong>The two \"eva\"s.</strong> Verse 12's <em>sa eva</em> (\"He alone\" is the one deluded by māyā and eating and drinking) and verse 19's <em>mayy eva</em> (\"in <strong>me</strong> alone is all born\") are the two ends of the same wire. Read them together and the Upaniṣad's thesis appears in a single sentence: <strong>the God you meditate on is the one wandering the three states, and that one is you.</strong>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Khaṇḍa 1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>  KAIVALYA UPANIṢAD — KHAṆḌA 1 : FROM WORSHIP TO IDENTITY\n\n  (1) ĀŚVALĀYANA ──asks──&gt; BRAHMĀ (Parameṣṭhin/Pitāmaha)\n        &quot;teach me brahmavidyā — highest · lineage-borne · hidden&quot;\n                    |\n  (2) THE ANSWER:   ŚRADDHĀ + BHAKTI + DHYĀNA  ──(one yoga)──&gt;  AVAIHI (&quot;realise it&quot;)\n                    |\n  (3-4) THE GATE:   not KARMA · not PRAJĀ · not DHANA   --&gt;   TYĀGA\n                    vedānta-vijñāna → suniścita-artha → saṃnyāsa-yoga → śuddha-sattva\n                    |\n  ┌─────────────── ONE SENTENCE (5 → 7) ───────────────┐\n  │ (5) SEAT:  solitary place · easy posture · pure ·   │\n  │            head-neck-body even · atyāśramin ·       │\n  │            senses restrained · GURU saluted (bhaktyā)│\n  │ (6) PLACE: HṚT-PUṆḌARĪKA — dust-free, pure          │\n  │            in its middle: bright · sorrowless ·     │\n  │            unthinkable · unmanifest · endless-formed│\n  │            · śiva · tranquil · immortal · brahma-yoni│\n  │ (7) OBJECT: ┌ absolute: ādi-madhya-anta-vihīna,     │\n  │             │           eka · vibhu · CIDĀNANDA ·   │\n  │             │           arūpa · adbhuta             │\n  │             └ iconic:   UMĀ-SAHĀYA · Parameśvara ·  │\n  │                         TRILOCANA · NĪLAKAṆṬHA      │\n  │  ==&gt; dhyātvā MUNIR GACCHATI:  bhūta-yoni ·          │\n  │        SAMASTA-SĀKṢIN · tamasaḥ parastāt            │\n  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                    |\n  (8-9) HE IS ALL:  Brahmā · Śiva · Indra · akṣara · Viṣṇu · prāṇa ·\n                    Kāla · Agni · Candramas · all that was &amp; will be\n                    &quot;jñātvā taṃ mṛtyum atyeti — NĀNYAḤ PANTHĀ&quot;\n  (10) DOUBLE VISION:  Self in all beings  &lt;—&gt;  all beings in the Self\n  (11) THE ARAṆI:   self = lower stick | OM = upper stick | ABHYĀSA = friction\n                    --&gt; burns PĀPA (var. PĀŚA, the noose)\n\n  ── THE THREE STATES (12-14) ──────────────────────────\n     JĀGRAT (12)   gross objects: strī·anna·pāna  -&gt; paritṛpti\n     SVAPNA (13)   world &quot;kalpita by his OWN māyā&quot; -&gt; sukha-duḥkha\n     SUṢUPTI (13)  all dissolved · TAMO-&#x27;BHIBHŪTAḤ  -&gt; sukha-rūpa\n        ↑ the SAME jīva (sa eva), driven by janmāntara-karma (14)\n        PURATRAYA — &quot;the three cities&quot; in which the jīva PLAYS\n             ↓ all three arise from &amp; dissolve into ↓\n        ĀDHĀRA · ĀNANDA · AKHAṆḌA-BODHA\n  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  (15) and from THAT: prāṇa · manas · senses | kha · vāyu · jyotis · āpas · pṛthvī\n\n  (16) ★ TAT TVAM EVA — TVAM EVA TAT ★   (the equation, stated both ways)\n\n  (17) That which ILLUMINES waking/dream/sleep = &quot;TAD BRAHMĀHAM&quot; -&gt; all bonds fall\n  (18) beyond the TRIPUTĪ (bhogya · bhoktṛ · bhoga):\n            SĀKṢĪ · CINMĀTRO &#x27;HAM · SADĀŚIVAḤ\n  (19) ★ MAYI born · MAYI established · MAYI dissolved ★\n            TAD BRAHMĀDVAYAM ASMY AHAM</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Khaṇḍa 1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Ask properly, and ask the highest</strong> (1). The text begins with an <em>approach</em>, not a technique. Before method, settle the question: do you actually want the knowledge that ends seeking, or relief that lets seeking continue? The Upaniṣad answers only the first question.","2. <strong>Take all three strands, not one</strong> (2). <em>Śraddhā</em> without <em>dhyāna</em> is sentiment; <em>dhyāna</em> without <em>bhakti</em> is dry technique; <em>bhakti</em> without <em>śraddhā</em> has nothing to stand on. Brahmā gives them as one ablative. Check which of the three you habitually skip — that is your work.","3. <strong>Audit the three goods</strong> (3). Work, family, wealth. The verse does not forbid them; it says they do not deliver <em>amṛtatva</em>. Spend a week noticing how much of your effort is quietly hoping one of them will.","4. <strong>Fix the seat before the meditation</strong> (5). Solitary place, a posture you can actually hold, cleanliness, and above all the <strong>even line of head, neck and trunk</strong>. Then bow — to your teacher, or to the lineage that carried this to you. The salutation is part of the technique, not decoration.","5. <strong>Clean the lotus first, then install the Lord</strong> (6). Do not begin with the formless. Begin by attending at the heart until the region feels <em>viraja</em> and <em>viśuddha</em> — dust-free, bright — and only then let the object appear in its middle. Hold it as the verse describes it: vivid <em>and</em> dissolving.","6. <strong>Meditate on the form until the form looks back</strong> (7). Use Umā-sahāya, three-eyed, blue-throated — or whatever form your devotion actually answers to. The Upaniṣad's move is not to abandon the form but to discover it as <em>samasta-sākṣin</em>, the Witness of all. Meditate on the God until you notice you are meditating <em>from</em> Him.","7. <strong>Turn the sticks daily and without drama</strong> (11). <em>Abhyāsa</em> is the operative word: OM, held steadily, day after day. Fire is not produced by one heroic effort but by refusing to stop rubbing. Expect heat long before flame.","8. <strong>Run the three-state enquiry in your own life</strong> (12–14). Each night: <em>what was I in waking? in dream? in deep sleep?</em> Each morning: <em>who reports that the sleep was sound?</em> Nothing here has to be believed — it can be checked.","9. <strong>Say the equation in both directions</strong> (16). <em>Tat tvam eva, tvam eva tat.</em> One direction makes a promise; both directions make an identity.","10. <strong>Practise the Witness in short, exact doses</strong> (17–18). Not \"I am the Absolute\" — too large to be honest. Try instead: <em>whatever is being experienced, I am what it is appearing to</em>. Ten seconds at a time, many times a day.","11. <strong>End with <em>mayi</em>, not <em>mayā</strong></em> (19). At the close of practice, do not affirm that you <em>made</em> the world; notice that it <em>arises in</em> you — appears, persists, subsides — while you neither begin nor end with it."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Khaṇḍa 2 — The Hymn of \"I Am,\" and the Fruit","u":[{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 2","title":"The Hymn of \"I Am,\" and the Fruit","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The second khaṇḍa has no new doctrine. It has a new</em> <strong>voice</strong>. <em>Everything established in the first khaṇḍa is now simply</em> <strong>spoken from the inside</strong>: <em>I am subtler than the subtle and vaster than the vast (20); I act without organs — seeing eyeless, hearing earless, and there is no one who knows me (21); by all the Vedas am I to be known, and I am their author and their knower; there is no merit or demerit for me, no destruction, no birth, no body, senses or intellect (22); I am not earth, water, fire, air, or space (23). Then the text turns outward once more for its</em> <strong>phala-śruti</strong> <em>— the Śatarudriya, Avimukta, the</em> atyāśramin<em>, and the twice-spoken promise of the</em> <strong>state of Aloneness</strong> <em>(24).</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Verse 20","title":"\"Subtler than the subtle am I; I am the vast\"","sa":["अणोरणीयानहमेव तद्वन्महानहं विश्वमहं विचित्रम् ।","पुरातनोऽहं पुरुषोऽहमीशो हिरण्मयोऽहं शिवरूपमस्मि ॥ २० ॥"],"iast":["aṇor aṇīyān aham eva tadvan mahān ahaṃ viśvam ahaṃ vicitram |","purātano 'haṃ puruṣo 'ham īśo hiraṇmayo 'haṃ śiva-rūpam asmi || 20 ||"],"words":[["aṇoḥ aṇīyān aham eva","m. abl. + compar. m. nom. + nom.","I alone am subtler than the atom (the subtle)"],["tadvat mahān aham","adv. + m. nom.","likewise I am the great (vast)"],["viśvam ahaṃ vicitram","n. nom. ×2","I am the universe, the manifold"],["purātanaḥ aham","m. nom.","I am the ancient"],["puruṣaḥ aham īśaḥ","m. nom. ×3","I am the Person, the Lord"],["hiraṇmayaḥ aham","m. nom.","I am the golden one"],["śiva-rūpam asmi","n. nom. + pres. 1 sg.","I am of the nature of Śiva (of auspicious form)"]],"lit":"\"I alone am subtler than the subtle; likewise I am the vast. I am the universe, the manifold. I am the ancient one; I am the Person, the Lord; I am the golden; I am of the form of Śiva.\"","para":"The great <strong>aham-hymn</strong> begins, and it begins by taking the most famous pair of epithets in the Upaniṣads — <strong><em>aṇor aṇīyān mahato mahīyān</em></strong>, \"subtler than the subtle, vaster than the vast\" (Kaṭha 1.2.20, Śvet. 3.20) — and putting them in the <strong>first person</strong>. Everything that the earlier texts said <em>about</em> the Self, this text says <em>as</em> the Self. The sequence is deliberate: first the two <strong>extremes of scale</strong> (subtler than the atom; vast), which together abolish size as a category; then <strong><em>viśvam ahaṃ vicitram</em></strong> — \"I am the universe, the manifold\" — which is the surprising line, since a Self \"subtler than the subtle\" might have been expected to <em>exclude</em> the manifold. It does not. Non-duality here is not the shrinking of the world to a point but the recognition that <strong>the variety is not other than me</strong>. Then four names of God, ending in <strong><em>śiva-rūpam asmi</em></strong> — and the neuter <em>rūpam</em> with the first-person verb is exact: not \"I am Śiva\" as one person claiming another's identity, but \"<strong>I am of the nature of the Auspicious</strong>.\"","coms":[["UBY","<em>aṇīyān</em> and <em>mahān</em> together deny all <em>parimāṇa</em> (measure); <em>viśvaṃ vicitram</em> is included to prevent the error of taking the Self as a <em>part</em> of the whole rather than its substrate."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>hiraṇmaya</em> recalls Īśa 15 (<em>hiraṇmayena pātreṇa</em>) and the <em>hiraṇmaya puruṣa</em> of Chāndogya 1.6 — the Self as the golden Person in the sun and in the eye."],["Chinmayānanda","the hymn is not arrogance but the <strong>removal of a limit</strong>. The ego says \"I am this much\"; the Upaniṣad simply keeps deleting the \"this much\" until only \"I am\" is left."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","these four verses (20–23) are given for <em>nididhyāsana</em> — to be recited aloud, slowly, as the declaration of what one has understood, until the mind stops objecting."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","the variant <em>purāṇo 'ham</em> for <em>purātano 'ham</em> is well attested and metrically smoother; the <em>aham</em>-style hymn links this text to the <em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em> tradition and to the later <em>Avadhūta</em> and <em>Amṛtabindu</em> literature."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 21","title":"Seeing without eyes, and known by none","sa":["अपाणिपादोऽहमचिन्त्यशक्तिः पश्याम्यचक्षुः स शृणोम्यकर्णः ।","अहं विजानामि विविक्तरूपो न चास्ति वेत्ता मम चित्सदाऽहम् ॥ २१ ॥"],"iast":["apāṇi-pādo 'ham acintya-śaktiḥ paśyāmy acakṣuḥ sa śṛṇomy akarṇaḥ |","ahaṃ vijānāmi vivikta-rūpo na cāsti vettā mama cit sadā 'ham || 21 ||"],"words":[["a-pāṇi-pādaḥ aham","bahuvrīhi m. nom.","without hands and feet am I"],["acintya-śaktiḥ","bahuvrīhi m. nom.","(yet) of inconceivable power"],["paśyāmi acakṣuḥ","pres. 1 sg. + adj.","I see, (though) eyeless"],["sa śṛṇomi akarṇaḥ","nom. + pres. 1 sg. + adj.","I hear, (though) earless"],["ahaṃ vijānāmi vivikta-rūpaḥ","nom. + pres. 1 sg. + m. nom.","I know, (being) of distinct (unmixed) nature"],["na ca asti vettā mama","+ pres. + m. nom. + gen.","and there exists no knower of me"],["cit sadā aham","f./n. + adv. + nom.","I am consciousness, always"]],"lit":"\"Without hands or feet am I, of inconceivable power; I see without eyes, I hear without ears. I know, being of unmixed nature, and there is no knower of me. I am consciousness, always.\"","para":"Built on <strong>Śvetāśvatara 3.19</strong> (<em>apāṇipādo javano grahītā paśyaty acakṣuḥ sa śṛṇoty akarṇaḥ</em>) — again transposed into the first person. The logic is precise: the organs are <strong>instruments of the empirical person</strong>, not of consciousness; awareness does not <em>acquire</em> seeing from the eye, it <strong>lends</strong> seeing to it. Hence \"eyeless, I see.\" The pivot is <strong><em>acintya-śakti</em></strong>: a power that cannot be conceived, because to conceive it would be to make it an object — and the verse immediately states why that is impossible: <strong><em>na cāsti vettā mama</em></strong> — \"<strong>there is no knower of me.</strong>\" This is the Upaniṣad's sharpest single line, and it is the same point as Bṛhadāraṇyaka's <em>vijñātāram are kena vijānīyāt</em>: the knower can never be known, since anything known is thereby not the knower. And <strong><em>vivikta-rūpa</em></strong> deliberately answers verse 5's <strong><em>vivikta-deśa</em></strong>: the seeker sat in a \"solitary place\"; he has turned out to <em>be</em> the solitary one. That is <em>kaivalya</em>.","coms":[["UBY","<em>vivikta-rūpa</em> = unmixed with the <em>upādhis</em>; the denial of a knower establishes the Self as <em>svayaṃ-prakāśa</em> (self-luminous), needing no other cognition to be established."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the contradiction \"eyeless yet seeing\" is resolved by distinguishing <em>sākṣi-caitanya</em> (which never acts) from <em>vṛtti-jñāna</em> (which does); the Self \"sees\" as the light in which seeing occurs."],["Chinmayānanda","this is why the Self is never <em>found</em> as a result of searching — every search looks for an object, and the seeker is what is looking. The verse closes the door on objectification for good."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","in practice, whenever the mind asks \"have I attained it yet?\", recall <em>na cāsti vettā mama</em> — the very question presumes a second who could certify the first."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","a first-person recasting of Śvet. 3.19; the loose <em>sa</em> in <em>sa śṛṇomi</em> is an artefact of the borrowing (the source has third-person <em>śṛṇoti</em>) and is retained in the vulgate."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 22","title":"\"By all the Vedas am I to be known\"","sa":["वेदैरनेकैरहमेव वेद्यो वेदान्तकृद्वेदविदेव चाहम् ।","न पुण्यपापे मम नास्ति नाशो न जन्म देहेन्द्रियबुद्धिरस्ति ॥ २२ ॥"],"iast":["vedair anekair aham eva vedyo vedānta-kṛd veda-vid eva cāham |","na puṇya-pāpe mama nāsti nāśo na janma dehendriya-buddhir asti || 22 ||"],"words":[["vedaiḥ anekaiḥ aham eva vedyaḥ","m. instr. pl. + nom. + gerundive m. nom.","by the many Vedas it is I alone who am to be known"],["vedānta-kṛt veda-vit eva ca aham","m. nom. ×2 + nom.","and I alone am the maker of Vedānta and the knower of the Veda"],["na puṇya-pāpe mama","n. nom. du. + gen.","there is no merit or demerit for me"],["na asti nāśaḥ","+ pres. + m. nom.","there is no destruction"],["na janma","n. nom.","no birth"],["deha-indriya-buddhiḥ asti","f. nom. + pres.","(nor) body, senses, or intellect"]],"lit":"\"By all the many Vedas it is I alone who am to be known; and I alone am the author of Vedānta and the knower of the Veda. For me there is neither merit nor demerit, no destruction, no birth, no body, no senses, no intellect.\"","para":"Two claims, both startling, and the second follows from the first. (a) <strong><em>Vedair anekair aham eva vedyaḥ</em></strong> — the entire Veda, in all its branches, has <strong>one referent</strong>, and that referent is the speaker; moreover he is <em>both</em> <strong>vedānta-kṛt</strong> (the one who makes/completes Vedānta) and <strong>veda-vit</strong> (the one who knows the Veda) — subject, object and author of scripture at once. (The line is near-identical to <strong>Gītā 15.15</strong>, <em>vedaiś ca sarvair aham eva vedyo vedānta-kṛd veda-vid eva cāham</em>, where Kṛṣṇa speaks it — a striking cross-tradition witness to a shared formula.) (b) The consequence: if I am what the Veda is about, then <strong>the Veda's whole apparatus of merit and demerit does not apply to me.</strong> <em>Puṇya</em> and <em>pāpa</em> belong to an agent, and the agent is the body-senses-intellect, which — the verse ends flatly — <strong>I do not have</strong>. This is not antinomianism; it is the precise Vedāntic statement that <strong>ethics governs the person while ignorance lasts, and the Self was never the person</strong>. Verse 3 said immortality is not reached <em>by works</em>; verse 22 says why: works and their fruits never touched the one who was seeking.","coms":[["UBY","<em>vedānta-kṛt</em> is glossed as the one who <em>brings the Veda to its end (anta)</em>, i.e. in whom all enquiry terminates; the denial of <em>puṇya-pāpa</em> applies to the <em>paramārtha</em> standpoint only, and never licenses the <em>ajñānin</em>."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the denial-series (<em>nāśa, janma, deha, indriya, buddhi</em>) is the classical <em>neti-neti</em> run in the first person; each item denied is one of the six <em>ūrmis</em> or <em>koṣas</em> of the empirical individual."],["Chinmayānanda","every scripture is a finger; this verse says the finger points at the reader. The shock is intended — the student who has followed vv. 1–21 is meant to feel it."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","the verse is a <em>jīvanmukta</em>'s statement, not a beginner's license. Practise the denial inwardly; observe the disciplines outwardly. Both are true at their own level."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","the close parallel with Bhagavad-Gītā 15.15 is the clearest instance of shared formulaic material between Kaivalya and the Gītā; direction of borrowing is undetermined, and a common source is likely."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 23","title":"Not the elements; and the entry into the pure Self","sa":["न भूमिरापो न च वह्निरस्ति न चानिलो मेऽस्ति न चाम्बरं च ।","एवं विदित्वा परमात्मरूपं गुहाशयं निष्कलमद्वितीयम् ॥ २३ ॥","समस्तसाक्षिं सदसद्विहीनं प्रयाति शुद्धं परमात्मरूपम् ॥"],"iast":["na bhūmir āpo na ca vahnir asti na cānilo me 'sti na cāmbaraṃ ca |","evaṃ viditvā paramātma-rūpaṃ guhāśayaṃ niṣkalam advitīyam ||","samasta-sākṣiṃ sad-asad-vihīnaṃ prayāti śuddhaṃ paramātma-rūpam || 23 ||"],"words":[["na bhūmiḥ āpaḥ na ca vahniḥ asti","f./f./m. nom. + pres.","for me there is no earth, no waters, no fire"],["na ca anilaḥ me asti","m. nom. + gen./dat.","nor is there air for me"],["na ca ambaram","n. nom.","nor space (ether)"],["evaṃ viditvā","adv. + abs.","thus having known"],["parama-ātma-rūpam","n. acc.","the form (nature) of the supreme Self"],["guhā-śayaṃ niṣkalam advitīyam","acc. ×3","resting in the cave, partless, without a second"],["samasta-sākṣim","m. acc.","the Witness of all"],["sat-asat-vihīnam","acc.","devoid of being and non-being"],["prayāti śuddhaṃ paramātma-rūpam","pres. + n. acc.","he attains the pure nature of the supreme Self"]],"lit":"\"For me there is no earth, no water, no fire, no air, no space. Thus having known the nature of the supreme Self — lying in the cave, partless, without a second, the Witness of all, devoid of being and non-being — one attains the pure nature of the supreme Self.\"","para":"The denial completes itself. Verse 22 removed the <strong>psycho-physical individual</strong> (body, senses, intellect, merit, demerit, birth, death); verse 23 removes the <strong>material universe</strong> by naming its five elements one by one — earth, water, fire, air, space. Between them, nothing empirical is left standing. And then the verse <strong>turns from \"I\" back to \"one\"</strong> and states the result in the third person, which is the correct grammar for a conclusion: <em>evaṃ viditvā... prayāti</em>. What is known is given five final epithets, and each closes a possible escape: <strong><em>guhāśaya</em></strong> (it is in the cave of <em>your</em> heart — not elsewhere), <strong><em>niṣkala</em></strong> (partless — it cannot be approached by degrees), <strong><em>advitīya</em></strong> (without a second — there is no observer left over), <strong><em>samasta-sākṣin</em></strong> (the Witness of all — it is not a blank), and <strong><em>sad-asad-vihīna</em></strong> (<strong>devoid of being and non-being</strong> — beyond the last pair of concepts, echoing the Nāsadīya hymn and Gītā 13.12). The attainment is of <strong><em>śuddhaṃ paramātma-rūpam</em></strong>, the <strong>pure</strong> nature of the supreme Self: pure meaning <em>unmixed</em>, which is precisely what <em>kevala</em> means.","coms":[["UBY","<em>sad-asad-vihīna</em> denies the applicability of <em>existence</em> and <em>non-existence</em> as understood of objects; the Self is not a <em>bhāva</em> among <em>bhāvas</em>. <em>Niṣkala</em> excludes the Sāṃkhya <em>kalā</em>s and the tantric <em>kalā</em>s alike."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the elemental negation completes the <em>pañca-koṣa-viveka</em>: having denied the sheaths inwardly (v. 22), the text denies their material constituents outwardly."],["Chinmayānanda","note that the negation is not a denial that the world appears, but of its being <em>mine</em> — <em>me 'sti</em>, \"there is (none) <em>for me</em>.\" The world remains; the ownership goes."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","the classical <em>bhūta-śuddhi</em> / <em>laya</em> meditation in negative form: dissolve earth, water, fire, air, space, and rest in the partless."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","editions divide here — some print the extra half-verse (<em>samasta-sākṣiṃ...</em>) as verse 24, making the text 25 verses; the vulgate followed here keeps it within 23 and numbers the closing prose 24."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 24","title":"The Śatarudriya, Avimukta, and the state of Aloneness","sa":["यः शतरुद्रियमधीते सोऽग्निपूतो भवति सुरापानात्पूतो भवति","स ब्रह्महत्यायाः पूतो भवति स सुवर्णस्तेयात्पूतो भवति","स कृत्याकृत्यात्पूतो भवति तस्मादविमुक्तमाश्रितो","भवत्यत्याश्रमी सर्वदा सकृद्वा जपेत् ॥","अनेन ज्ञानमाप्नोति संसारार्णवनाशनम् । तस्मादेवं","विदित्वैनं कैवल्यं पदमश्नुते कैवल्यं पदमश्नुत इति ॥ २४ ॥","ॐ सह नाववतु । सह नौ भुनक्तु । सह वीर्यं करवावहै ।","तेजस्वि नावधीतमस्तु । मा विद्विषावहै ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥","इत्यथर्ववेदीया कैवल्योपनिषत्समाप्ता ॥"],"iast":["yaḥ śatarudriyam adhīte so 'gni-pūto bhavati surāpānāt pūto bhavati","sa brahmahatyāyāḥ pūto bhavati sa suvarṇa-steyāt pūto bhavati","sa kṛtyākṛtyāt pūto bhavati tasmād avimuktam āśrito","bhavaty atyāśramī sarvadā sakṛd vā japet ||","anena jñānam āpnoti saṃsārārṇava-nāśanam | tasmād evaṃ","viditvainaṃ kaivalyaṃ padam aśnute kaivalyaṃ padam aśnuta iti || 24 ||","ity atharvavedīyā kaivalyopaniṣat samāptā ||"],"words":[["yaḥ śatarudriyam adhīte","rel. nom. + n. acc. + pres. mid.","he who recites (studies) the Śatarudriya"],["saḥ agni-pūtaḥ bhavati","nom. + pp. + pres.","he becomes purified by fire"],["surā-pānāt pūtaḥ bhavati","abl.","he becomes purified from (the sin of) liquor-drinking"],["sa brahma-hatyāyāḥ pūtaḥ","f. abl.","purified from brahmin-slaying"],["sa suvarṇa-steyāt pūtaḥ","n. abl.","purified from the theft of gold"],["sa kṛtya-akṛtyāt pūtaḥ","n. abl.","purified from (the sin of) the done and the undone"],["tasmāt avimuktam āśritaḥ bhavati","abl. + m. acc. + pp. + pres.","therefore he becomes one who has resorted to Avimukta"],["atyāśramī sarvadā sakṛt vā japet","m. nom. + adv. + adv. + opt. 3 sg.","the atyāśramin should recite (it) always, or at least once"],["anena jñānam āpnoti","instr. + n. acc. + pres.","by this he obtains the knowledge"],["saṃsāra-arṇava-nāśanam","n. acc.","that destroys the ocean of saṃsāra"],["tasmāt evaṃ viditvā enam","abl. + adv. + abs. + m. acc.","therefore, having thus known Him,"],["kaivalyaṃ padam aśnute (bis)","n. acc. ×2 + pres. mid.","he attains the state of Aloneness (said twice)"]],"lit":"\"He who recites the Śatarudriya becomes purified as by fire; he is purified of liquor-drinking, purified of brahmin-slaying, purified of the theft of gold, purified of what has been done and left undone. Therefore he becomes one who has taken refuge in Avimukta. The <em>atyāśramin</em> should recite it always, or at least once. By this he gains the knowledge that destroys the ocean of <em>saṃsāra</em>. Therefore, having thus known Him, he attains the state of Aloneness — he attains the state of Aloneness.\"","para":"The <strong>phala-śruti</strong>, and it is pure Śaiva liturgy: the <strong><em>Śatarudriya</em></strong> — the \"Hundred (names) of Rudra,\" the great litany of <em>Taittirīya Saṃhitā</em> 4.5 (the <strong>Rudram</strong> recited daily in Śaiva worship to this day) — is prescribed as the text's ritual companion. The four sins listed are the classical <strong><em>mahāpātakas</em></strong> of the Dharmaśāstras (drinking liquor, killing a brahmin, stealing gold — the fourth, adultery with the guru's wife, is here replaced by the comprehensive <strong><em>kṛtyākṛtya</em></strong>, \"what was done and what was left undone,\" which sweeps in sins of omission as well). <strong><em>Avimukta</em></strong> is the sacred precinct of <strong>Vārāṇasī/Kāśī</strong>, \"the never-forsaken,\" where Śiva is said never to abandon the dying — and the phrase <em>avimuktam āśritaḥ</em> carries a second sense the Upaniṣad surely intends: <strong>he becomes one who has taken refuge in the Never-Released, the unliberated-because-never-bound</strong>. The <strong>atyāśramin</strong> of verse 5 returns to close the ring. And the final words are <strong>doubled</strong> — <em>kaivalyaṃ padam aśnute kaivalyaṃ padam aśnute</em> — the standard Upaniṣadic signal that <strong>the text is complete</strong>; the repetition is the scriptural full stop.","coms":[["UBY","<em>avimukta</em> is read both geographically (Kāśī) and etymologically (<em>a-vimukta</em>, the never-abandoned — the space between the brows, in another traditional gloss, where Śiva dwells as <em>avimukteśvara</em>); the <em>phala-śruti</em> is for the <em>adhikārin</em> who is not yet ripe for pure <em>jñāna</em>."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the purificatory claims belong to the <em>karma-kāṇḍa</em> register and serve <em>citta-śuddhi</em>; the <em>jñāna</em> of the last line is the true fruit, and the doubled ending marks the end of the <em>śāstra</em>."],["Chinmayānanda","the recitation is for the mind that still needs a ritual handhold; its point is <em>anena jñānam āpnoti</em> — the <em>japa</em> is a means to the knowledge, never a substitute for it."],["Sivānanda/Krishnānanda","recite the Rudram, live at Avimukta (outwardly or in the heart), and hold the teaching of the twenty-three verses — the three together are the complete discipline this Upaniṣad prescribes."],["Deussen/Aiyar/Olivelle","the prose <em>phala-śruti</em> is very likely a later liturgical accretion attaching the Upaniṣad to Śatarudriya recitation and to the Kāśī cult; its idiom and its Dharmaśāstric sin-list differ markedly from the verse portion."]],"notes":["॥ द्वितीयः खण्डः ॥ २ ॥ — <em>Thus the second khaṇḍa.</em>"]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Khaṇḍa 2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The second khaṇḍa is the first khaṇḍa <strong>spoken from the other side.</strong> Five observations:","1. <strong>It is a hymn, not an argument.</strong> Verses 20–23 prove nothing; they <em>declare</em>. This is deliberate. Argument belongs to the stage where a seeker is being persuaded (vv. 12–16); declaration belongs to the stage where what was understood is being <strong>occupied</strong>. The technical name for this stage is <em>nididhyāsana</em>, and the Upaniṣad supplies its text.","2. <strong>The three negations, in order.</strong> Verse 21 denies the <strong>organs</strong> (hands, feet, eyes, ears) while affirming their functions; verse 22 denies the <strong>person</strong> (merit, demerit, death, birth, body, senses, intellect); verse 23 denies the <strong>world</strong> (the five elements). Nothing that could be pointed at survives — and the Witness, who cannot be pointed at, is what remains.","3. <strong>The one positive claim.</strong> Amid all the negation stands <em>viśvam ahaṃ vicitram</em> (20): \"<strong>I am the universe, the manifold.</strong>\" It is essential. Without it, the hymn would be a doctrine of withdrawal — the puruṣa of Sāṃkhya extracting itself from prakṛti, which is exactly the <em>kaivalya</em> the Yoga-sūtra teaches. With it, the meaning shifts: the manifold is not escaped but <strong>reclaimed</strong>. <em>This</em> is how the title-word is re-minted.","4. <strong>\"There is no knower of me\" (21) is the load-bearing line.</strong> It is the reason the Self cannot be attained as a result, the reason the Veda can only point (22), and the reason the elements' denial (23) leaves no observer behind. Any spiritual ambition that survives the first khaṇḍa dies here.","5. <strong>The close returns to the world of practice.</strong> The last verse is liturgy, sin-lists, a pilgrimage city, a recommendation to recite. Modern readers often find this an anticlimax after v. 23; it is better read as the text's honesty about its <strong>two audiences</strong> — the <em>atyāśramin</em> who has understood, and the reciter who has not yet, for whom <em>japa</em> and the Rudram are the road. The Upaniṣad addresses both without embarrassment, and gives them the same last line.","<strong>On the word <em>kaivalya</em>.</strong> The text ends with the word it is named for, and it is worth tracking what has happened to it. In <strong>Sāṃkhya-Yoga</strong> (<em>Yoga-sūtra</em> 2.25, 4.34), <em>kaivalya</em> is the <strong>isolation of puruṣa from prakṛti</strong>: the two are real and eternally distinct, and liberation is the ending of their false conjunction. Consciousness is left <em>alone</em> — but alone <em>alongside</em> an abandoned nature. This Upaniṣad keeps the word and breaks the dualism. Here aloneness is not achieved by separation, because — <em>mayy eva sakalaṃ jātam</em> (19), <em>viśvam ahaṃ vicitram</em> (20) — <strong>there is nothing to separate from</strong>. <em>Kaivalya</em> becomes a synonym of <em>advaita</em>: not <em>\"only I, and not that,\"</em> but <strong><em>\"only That, which is I.\"</em></strong> The title is thus a quiet polemic, and the whole text is its argument."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Khaṇḍa 2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>  KAIVALYA UPANIṢAD — KHAṆḌA 2 : THE HYMN OF &quot;I AM&quot;\n\n        (v.16  TAT TVAM EVA · TVAM EVA TAT)\n                       |\n                       v   the voice changes: third person --&gt; FIRST person\n  ╔══════════════════ THE AHAM-HYMN (20-23) ══════════════════╗\n  ║ (20) SCALE ABOLISHED                                       ║\n  ║       aṇor aṇīyān AHAM   ·   mahān AHAM                    ║\n  ║       ★ VIŚVAM AHAṂ VICITRAM ★  (the manifold = me)        ║\n  ║       purātana · puruṣa · īśa · hiraṇmaya · ŚIVA-RŪPAM ASMI║\n  ║                                                            ║\n  ║ (21) ORGANS DENIED, FUNCTION AFFIRMED                      ║\n  ║       apāṇipāda  but  ACINTYA-ŚAKTI                        ║\n  ║       eyeless -&gt; I SEE   |   earless -&gt; I HEAR             ║\n  ║       vivikta-rūpa (&quot;the SOLITARY one&quot; &lt;- vivikta-deśa v.5)║\n  ║       ★ NA CĀSTI VETTĀ MAMA ★  &quot;there is no knower of me&quot;  ║\n  ║       cit sadā aham                                        ║\n  ║                                                            ║\n  ║ (22) THE PERSON DENIED                                     ║\n  ║       &quot;by all the VEDAS am I to be known&quot;                  ║\n  ║       vedānta-kṛt + veda-vid      (cf. Gītā 15.15)         ║\n  ║       no puṇya · no pāpa · no nāśa · no janma ·            ║\n  ║       no deha · indriya · buddhi                           ║\n  ║                                                            ║\n  ║ (23) THE WORLD DENIED                                      ║\n  ║       na bhūmiḥ · na āpaḥ · na vahniḥ · na anilaḥ · na ambaram\n  ║       --&gt; guhāśaya · NIṢKALA · ADVITĪYA ·                  ║\n  ║           SAMASTA-SĀKṢIN · sad-asad-vihīna                 ║\n  ║       ==&gt; prayāti ŚUDDHAṂ PARAMĀTMA-RŪPAM                  ║\n  ╚════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝\n                       |\n  (24) PHALA-ŚRUTI — back to the world of practice\n       recite the ŚATARUDRIYA (Rudram, TS 4.5)\n         purified as by fire, from surāpāna · brahmahatyā ·\n         suvarṇasteya · kṛtya-akṛtya (the mahāpātakas)\n       --&gt; &quot;resorted to AVIMUKTA&quot;  (Kāśī  ||  the NEVER-FORSAKEN)\n       the ATYĀŚRAMIN (v.5 returns) recites always, or at least ONCE\n       --&gt; anena JÑĀNAM āpnoti — saṃsāra-ārṇava-nāśanam\n       ★ KAIVALYAṂ PADAM AŚNUTE  ·  KAIVALYAṂ PADAM AŚNUTE ★\n              (doubled = the scripture is complete)\n\n  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  THE WORD ITSELF:\n     SĀṂKHYA-YOGA kaivalya = puruṣa ISOLATED FROM prakṛti  (two reals)\n     KAIVALYA UP.  kaivalya = advaya — nothing to be isolated FROM\n                              (19) mayy eva sakalaṃ jātam\n                              (20) viśvam ahaṃ vicitram\n  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Khaṇḍa 2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Recite the hymn aloud</strong> (20–23). These four verses are meant to be <em>spoken</em>, slowly, in the first person, at the end of meditation — not analysed. Let the mind object; keep going. This is the prescribed form of <em>nididhyāsana</em> in this text.","2. <strong>Keep the manifold in</strong> (20). If your practice is making the world thinner, flatter, less interesting, check it against <em>viśvam ahaṃ vicitram</em>. Kaivalya is not withdrawal from variety; it is the discovery that variety was never separate.","3. <strong>Use \"no knower of me\" against spiritual ambition</strong> (21). Whenever practice becomes a project with a progress bar — <em>am I getting closer?</em> — apply the line. The question posits a second self, standing outside, grading the first. There is no such person.","4. <strong>Notice the functions without the organs</strong> (21). A small daily exercise: while seeing, ask what it is <em>in which</em> the seeing is occurring. Not the eye — the eye is seen. Stay with what is not seen.","5. <strong>Separate the levels honestly</strong> (22). At the level of the Witness, no merit or demerit. At the level of the person, every bit of it applies. Confusing them in either direction is the classic error — asceticism that thinks the Self needs purifying, or licence that thinks the person does not.","6. <strong>Run the elemental dissolution</strong> (23). Earth, water, fire, air, space — take them in order, in the body and in the world, and let each go. End in <em>niṣkala</em>, the partless, where there is nothing further to take apart.","7. <strong>Take the last verse seriously, in its own register</strong> (24). If devotion is alive in you, recite the <strong>Śatarudriya</strong>. The Upaniṣad, having just said that no ritual reaches the Self, still recommends this one — because the mind that recites is the mind that must eventually sit still, and it must be brought along, not scolded.","8. <strong>Note where \"Avimukta\" really is.</strong> Kāśī, yes — but the commentators' second reading is the practical one: take refuge in <em>the never-forsaken</em>, what has never let you go and has never been bound. That refuge is available where you are sitting.","9. <strong>Let the doubled ending do its work.</strong> <em>Kaivalyaṃ padam aśnute, kaivalyaṃ padam aśnute.</em> The repetition is the tradition's way of saying: <strong>there is nothing after this.</strong> Close the book there, and sit."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Synthesis","title":"Details across the whole Upaniṣad","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>A complete sādhanā in twenty-four verses.</strong> No other short Upaniṣad moves through the entire path so economically: qualification (1–5) → seat and object of meditation (5–7) → the identity of the God with all gods and all beings (8–11) → the analysis of the three states (12–15) → the <em>mahāvākya</em> (16) → the Witness (17–18) → the seal of non-duality (19) → the hymn of \"I am\" (20–23) → the fruit (24). Each stage presupposes the last, and none is skipped. It can be read as a <strong>manual</strong>, and the tradition has always read it so.","- <strong>The Śaiva–Vedāntic weld.</strong> The text's distinctive achievement is to hold together, without strain, two vocabularies that later traditions often set at odds: <strong>Umā-sahāya, Trilocana, Nīlakaṇṭha, Sadāśiva, Śatarudriya, Avimukta</strong> on one side; <strong><em>cidānanda, sākṣin, akhaṇḍa-bodha, advaya, tat tvam asi</em></strong> on the other. The weld is made at <strong>verse 18</strong> — <em>cinmātro 'haṃ sadāśivaḥ</em> — where the witness-consciousness of Advaita and the highest name of Śaivism are predicated of the same \"I.\" Everything before that verse prepares it; everything after it assumes it.","- <strong>Three movements of the pronoun.</strong> The text can be read by tracking one grammatical feature. It opens in the <strong>third person</strong> (<em>he approached, he said</em>), turns in v. 12 to the pointed <em>sa eva</em> (\"<strong>He alone</strong> is the one deluded by māyā\"), reaches the <strong>second person</strong> at v. 16 (<em>tvam eva tat</em>), and from v. 17 onward is entirely <strong>first person</strong> (<em>brahmāham, cinmātro 'ham, mayy eva, aham eva</em>). The grammar <em>is</em> the argument.","- <strong>Renunciation framing devotion framing knowledge.</strong> Vv. 3–5 make <em>tyāga</em> the gate; vv. 2, 5, 7 make <em>bhakti</em> the atmosphere; vv. 16–23 make <em>jñāna</em> the content. The text never suggests these compete. Its formula — <em>śraddhā-bhakti-dhyāna-yogāt</em> — treats them as one ablative.","- <strong>Two sources, visibly.</strong> The compiler works from <strong>Muṇḍaka</strong> (vv. 4, 15 are direct citations), <strong>Śvetāśvatara</strong> (vv. 9, 11, 21), <strong>Kaṭha</strong> (v. 20), the <strong>Mahānārāyaṇa / Taittirīya Āraṇyaka</strong> (v. 3), <strong>Chāndogya</strong> (vv. 6, 16), <strong>Māṇḍūkya</strong> (vv. 12–14, 17) and the <strong>Puruṣa-sūkta</strong> (vv. 8–9). What is original is not the material but the <strong>sequence</strong> — and the first-person hymn, which has no close Upaniṣadic precedent at this length.","- <strong>The redefinition of the title-word.</strong> The book's thesis is carried by its name. <em>Kaivalya</em>, the Sāṃkhya-Yoga term for the isolation of consciousness from nature, is retained and inverted: aloneness now means <strong>non-duality</strong>, since <em>\"in me all is born\"</em> (19) and <em>\"I am the manifold\"</em> (20) leave nothing outside to be isolated from. Read this way, the Upaniṣad is a sustained argument with Sāṃkhya conducted entirely by appropriating its vocabulary — the same strategy Śvetāśvatara uses with <em>prakṛti</em>, <em>guṇa</em> and <em>pradhāna</em>.","- <strong>What is unresolved.</strong> The prose <em>phala-śruti</em> (24) sits stylistically apart from the verses and is probably a liturgical addition; the Veda-attribution is genuinely contested (Atharvan colophon, Yajurvedic śānti and Muktikā placement); and the verse-count varies between 24 and 25 by where v. 23's closing half-verse is placed. None of these affects the doctrine, and all are worth knowing before quoting the text by number."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"the whole Upaniṣad","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>              KAIVALYA UPANIṢAD — THE WHOLE ARC\n\n  ĀŚVALĀYANA ─────ask─────&gt; BRAHMĀ\n       |\n       |  (2)  śraddhā · bhakti · dhyāna ......... THE METHOD\n       |  (3-4) not karma/prajā/dhana → TYĀGA .... THE GATE\n       |  (5)  seat · guru ...................... THE BODY\n       |  (6)  hṛt-puṇḍarīka .................... THE PLACE\n       v\n  ┌──────────── (7) UMĀ-SAHĀYA PARAMEŚVARA ────────────┐\n  │   absolute AND iconic; meditated in the heart      │ WORSHIP\n  └────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┘\n                           |\n       (8-9)  He is Brahmā · Śiva · Indra · Viṣṇu · prāṇa · kāla · agni · soma\n       (10)   Self in all beings  &lt;—&gt;  all beings in Self\n       (11)   ātman = araṇi | OM = uttarāraṇi | abhyāsa = friction\n                           |\n  ┌────────── (12-15) THE THREE STATES &amp; THEIR GROUND ──────────┐\n  │  jāgrat — svapna — suṣupti  =  PURATRAYA (&quot;three cities&quot;)   │ ENQUIRY\n  │  one jīva plays in all three; all three arise from &amp; sink   │\n  │  into: ĀDHĀRA · ĀNANDA · AKHAṆḌA-BODHA                      │\n  │  and from That: prāṇa, manas, indriyas; kha, vāyu,          │\n  │  jyotis, āpas, pṛthvī                                       │\n  └──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┘\n                             |\n              ★ (16) TAT TVAM EVA — TVAM EVA TAT ★      THE EQUATION\n                             |\n       (17) That which illumines all three states = I\n       (18) beyond bhogya/bhoktṛ/bhoga: SĀKṢĪ · CINMĀTRO &#x27;HAM · SADĀŚIVAḤ\n       (19) mayi jātam · mayi pratiṣṭhitam · mayi layam\n            ★ TAD BRAHMĀDVAYAM ASMY AHAM ★\n                             |\n  ┌────────── (20-23) THE HYMN OF &quot;I AM&quot; ──────────┐\n  │  scale denied · organs denied · person denied  │ IDENTITY\n  │  · elements denied ; VIŚVAM AHAṂ VICITRAM      │\n  │  &quot;na cāsti vettā mama&quot; ; śuddhaṃ paramātma-rūpam│\n  └────────────────────────┬───────────────────────┘\n                           |\n       (24) Śatarudriya · Avimukta · atyāśramin · japa\n            ★ KAIVALYAṂ PADAM AŚNUTE (×2) ★              THE FRUIT\n\n  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n   WORSHIP ──&gt; ENQUIRY ──&gt; EQUATION ──&gt; IDENTITY ──&gt; FRUIT\n   3rd person ──────&gt; &quot;sa eva&quot; ──&gt; &quot;tvam&quot; ──&gt; &quot;aham&quot; ──────────&gt;\n  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps and observations","title":"the whole text","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Read it as a sequence, not an anthology.</strong> The verses are ordered as a path. Taking v. 19 or v. 22 out of position — a common practice in quotation — yields either an inflated ego or a licence; taken in place, after the <em>tyāga</em> of v. 3 and the <em>sākṣī</em> of v. 18, they are exact.","2. <strong>Establish the three strands before the seat</strong> (2). Faith that steadies, devotion that warms, meditation that reveals. If practice has gone cold, the missing strand is usually <em>bhakti</em>; if it has gone vague, <em>dhyāna</em>; if it keeps restarting, <em>śraddhā</em>.","3. <strong>Use the heart-lotus as your fixed address</strong> (6). One place, returned to daily, becomes charged. The text's instruction is specific: <em>clean it first</em>, then install the Lord in the middle of it.","4. <strong>Meditate on a form you love, and let it become the Witness</strong> (7, 18). This is the text's signature move, and it is available to anyone with an <em>iṣṭa-devatā</em>. Worship the form until you find that the awareness in which it appears and the awareness it points to are one.","5. <strong>Check the three states nightly</strong> (12–14, 17). The only laboratory the teaching needs is your own sleep. Who wakes, who dreams, who sleeps, and who knows he slept well?","6. <strong>Say the equation in both directions, daily</strong> (16). <em>Tat tvam eva, tvam eva tat.</em>","7. <strong>Practise the Witness in short doses; recite the hymn in long ones</strong> (17–23). Witnessing is a matter of seconds repeated often; <em>nididhyāsana</em> is a matter of reciting vv. 20–23 slowly and letting them settle.","8. <strong>Distinguish <em>mayi</em> from <em>mayā</em>, and the two levels of v. 22.</strong> In me, not by me. No merit or demerit for the Self; all of it for the person. Keep both straight and the teaching is safe; confuse them and it becomes either anxiety or licence.","9. <strong>Give the liturgy its place</strong> (24). The Rudram, <em>japa</em>, the pilgrimage — the Upaniṣad ends with them after denying that anything of the kind reaches the Self. Both are true. Practise the ritual as the mind's road to stillness, and know it is a road, not the destination.","10. <strong>Let the doubled last line be the last word.</strong> <em>Kaivalyaṃ padam aśnute, kaivalyaṃ padam aśnute.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Bibliography","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<strong>Primary text &amp; translations</strong>","- <em>Kaivalyopaniṣat</em>, ITRANS e-text `kaivalya.itx` (transcribed and proofread by Sunder Hattangadi), sanskritdocuments.org — the base text for this edition.","- <strong>K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar</strong>, <em>Thirty Minor Upanishads</em> (Madras, 1914) — English translation of the Kaivalya with notes.","- <strong>Paul Deussen</strong>, <em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em> (Leipzig, 1897); Eng. tr. Bedekar &amp; Palsule, <em>Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda</em> (Delhi, 1980) — <em>Kaivalya-Upaniṣad</em> with introduction.","- <strong>Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin</strong>, <em>Dīpikā</em> on the 108 Upaniṣads, ed. Adyar Library (Madras) — the standard classical Sanskrit commentary on the minor Upaniṣads, including the Kaivalya.","- <strong>Swāmī Mādhavānanda / Advaita Ashrama</strong>, <em>Minor Upaniṣads</em> — text, translation and notes in the Advaita Ashrama series.","<strong>Commentary &amp; exposition</strong>","- <strong>Swāmī Chinmayānanda</strong>, <em>Kaivalya Upaniṣad</em> (Chinmaya Publications) — the best-known modern English commentary; a verse-by-verse sādhanā reading.","- <strong>Swāmī Sivānanda</strong>, <em>The Principal Upanishads</em> / <em>Ten Upanishads with Commentary</em>, and <strong>Swāmī Krishnānanda</strong>, lectures on the minor Upaniṣads (Divine Life Society, Rishikesh).","- <strong>Śaṅkarānanda</strong>, <em>Dīpikā</em> on the minor Upaniṣads (14th c.) — the Advaitic gloss tradition; note that some printed editions circulate the Kaivalya commentary under Śaṅkarācārya's name, an ascription modern scholarship rejects.","<strong>Context &amp; comparative</strong>","- <strong>Patrick Olivelle</strong>, <em>Saṃnyāsa Upaniṣads: Hindu Scriptures on Asceticism and Renunciation</em> (OUP, 1992), and <em>The Early Upaniṣads</em> (OUP, 1998) — for the renunciant milieu of vv. 3–5 and the Muṇḍaka/Chāndogya parallels.","- <strong>Jan Gonda</strong>, <em>Die Religionen Indiens</em> and <em>Viṣṇuism and Śivaism</em> — for the Umā-Maheśvara theology and the Śatarudriya cult.","- <strong>Śatarudriya</strong>: <em>Taittirīya Saṃhitā</em> 4.5 (the <em>Rudram</em>), with the <em>Camakam</em> (TS 4.7) — the liturgical text prescribed in v. 24."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-references within the Upaniṣadic & Vedic corpus","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Kaivalya locus</td><td>Parallel / source</td><td>Note</td></tr><tr><td>v. 2 <em>śraddhā-bhakti-dhyāna-yogāt</em></td><td>Śvetāśvatara 6.23 (<em>yasya deve parā bhaktiḥ</em>)</td><td>devotion as the condition of knowledge</td></tr><tr><td>v. 3 <em>na karmaṇā na prajayā dhanena</em></td><td>Mahānārāyaṇa Up. 10.5; Taittirīya Āraṇyaka 10.10</td><td>verbatim; the renunciant <em>locus classicus</em></td></tr><tr><td>v. 3 <em>nihitaṃ guhāyām</em></td><td>Kaṭha 1.2.12; Taittirīya 2.1</td><td>the Self in the cave of the heart</td></tr><tr><td>v. 4 <em>vedānta-vijñāna-suniścitārthāḥ</em></td><td><strong>Muṇḍaka 3.2.6</strong></td><td>verbatim citation; <em>krama-mukti</em></td></tr><tr><td>v. 5 <em>samagrīva-śiraḥ-śarīraḥ</em></td><td>Śvetāśvatara 2.8; Bhagavad-Gītā 6.13</td><td>the erect posture</td></tr><tr><td>v. 5 <em>atyāśrama-sthaḥ</em></td><td>Śvetāśvatara 6.21</td><td>the teaching given to <em>atyāśramins</em></td></tr><tr><td>v. 6 <em>hṛt-puṇḍarīkam</em></td><td>Chāndogya 8.1 (<em>dahara-vidyā</em>); Taittirīya 1.6</td><td>the heart-space as the place of meditation</td></tr><tr><td>v. 7 <em>tamasaḥ parastāt</em></td><td>Ṛgveda 10.90 / Śvetāśvatara 3.8 (<em>ādityavarṇaṃ tamasaḥ parastāt</em>)</td><td>beyond the darkness</td></tr><tr><td>v. 7 <em>umā-sahāyam</em></td><td>Kena 3–4 (Umā Haimavatī)</td><td>the earliest Upaniṣadic Umā</td></tr><tr><td>vv. 8–9 <em>sa eva sarvaṃ yad bhūtaṃ yac ca bhavyam</em></td><td>Ṛgveda 10.90.2 (Puruṣa-sūkta); Kaṭha 2.1.13</td><td>the Person as all that was and will be</td></tr><tr><td>v. 9 <em>jñātvā taṃ mṛtyum atyeti</em></td><td><strong>Śvetāśvatara 3.8</strong> (<em>nānyaḥ panthā vidyate 'yanāya</em>)</td><td>with <em>vimuktaye</em> for <em>ayanāya</em></td></tr><tr><td>v. 10 <em>sarva-bhūtastham ātmānam</em></td><td>Īśa 6; Bhagavad-Gītā 6.29</td><td>the double vision</td></tr><tr><td>v. 11 <em>ātmānam araṇiṃ kṛtvā</em></td><td><strong>Śvetāśvatara 1.14</strong> (<em>sva-deham araṇiṃ kṛtvā</em>)</td><td>the two fire-sticks</td></tr><tr><td>vv. 12–14 the three states</td><td>Māṇḍūkya 3–5; Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.3</td><td><em>viśva–taijasa–prājña</em>; the dream-world of one's own making</td></tr><tr><td>v. 14 <em>puratraya</em></td><td>Śvetāśvatara 3.18 (nine-gated city); the Tripura myth</td><td>the three cities as the three bodies</td></tr><tr><td>v. 15 <em>etasmāj jāyate prāṇaḥ</em></td><td><strong>Muṇḍaka 2.1.3</strong></td><td>verbatim cosmogony</td></tr><tr><td>v. 16 <em>tat tvam eva tvam eva tat</em></td><td><strong>Chāndogya 6.8–16</strong> (<em>tat tvam asi</em>)</td><td>the <em>mahāvākya</em>, stated both ways</td></tr><tr><td>v. 17 <em>yat prakāśate</em></td><td>Kaṭha 2.2.15; Muṇḍaka 2.2.10 (<em>tasya bhāsā sarvam idaṃ vibhāti</em>)</td><td>the self-luminous ground</td></tr><tr><td>v. 18 <em>sākṣī cinmātraḥ</em></td><td>Śvetāśvatara 6.11 (<em>sākṣī cetā kevalo nirguṇaś ca</em>)</td><td>the Witness — note <em>kevala</em></td></tr><tr><td>v. 19 <em>mayy eva sakalaṃ jātam</em></td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 9.4–6; Amṛtabindu, Ātmabodha</td><td>the world in, not by, the Self</td></tr><tr><td>v. 20 <em>aṇor aṇīyān</em></td><td><strong>Kaṭha 1.2.20</strong>; Śvetāśvatara 3.20</td><td>subtler than the subtle</td></tr><tr><td>v. 20 <em>hiraṇmayaḥ</em></td><td>Chāndogya 1.6.6; Īśa 15</td><td>the golden Person</td></tr><tr><td>v. 21 <em>apāṇipādo... paśyaty acakṣuḥ</em></td><td><strong>Śvetāśvatara 3.19</strong></td><td>in the first person here</td></tr><tr><td>v. 21 <em>na cāsti vettā mama</em></td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 2.4.14 / 4.5.15 (<em>vijñātāram are kena vijānīyāt</em>)</td><td>the knower cannot be known</td></tr><tr><td>v. 22 <em>vedaiś ca sarvair aham eva vedyaḥ</em></td><td><strong>Bhagavad-Gītā 15.15</strong></td><td>near-verbatim; shared formula</td></tr><tr><td>v. 23 <em>sad-asad-vihīnam</em></td><td>Ṛgveda 10.129.1 (Nāsadīya); Bhagavad-Gītā 13.12</td><td>beyond being and non-being</td></tr><tr><td>v. 23 <em>guhāśayaṃ niṣkalam</em></td><td>Muṇḍaka 3.1.7; Praśna 6.5</td><td>the partless one in the cave</td></tr><tr><td>v. 24 <em>śatarudriyam adhīte</em></td><td><strong>Taittirīya Saṃhitā 4.5</strong> (the <em>Rudram</em>)</td><td>the liturgical companion text</td></tr><tr><td>v. 24 <em>kaivalyaṃ padam</em></td><td>Yoga-sūtra 2.25, 4.34 (<em>kaivalya</em>)</td><td>the Sāṃkhya-Yoga term, here re-minted as <em>advaya</em></td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Colophon","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Thus the complete Kaivalya Upaniṣad — Āśvalāyana's question and Brahmā's four-word answer (1–2); renunciation as the gate (3–4); the seat, the heart-lotus, and the meditation on Umā's consort (5–7); the one God who is every god (8–9); the Self in all beings (10); the churning of knowledge with OM (11); the three states and the three cities, and the ground in which they dissolve (12–15); the equation stated both ways (16); the Witness beyond the three states (17–18); the seal of non-duality (19); the hymn of \"I am\" (20–23); and the Śatarudriya, Avimukta, and the twice-spoken fruit (24) — the</em> brahmavidyā <em>of the Aloneness that is not isolation but non-duality, concluded.</em>","<em>Oṃ saha nāvavatu… Oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ.</em>"],"kind":"note"}]}]};
